# Emerging Multifunctional Metal-Organic Framework Materials

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as coordination polymers, represent an interesting type of solid crystalline materials that can be straightforwardly self-assembled through the coordination of metal ions/clusters with organic linkers. Owing to the modular nature and mild conditions of MOF synthesis, the porosities of MOF materials can be systematically tuned by judicious selection of molecular building blocks, and a variety of functional sites/groups can be introduced into metal ions/clusters, organic linkers, or pore spaces through pre-designing or post-synthetic approaches. These unique advantages enable MOFs to be used as a highly versatile and tunable platform for exploring multifunctional MOF materials. Here, the bright potential of MOF materials as emerging multifunctional materials is highlighted in some of the most important applications for gas storage and separation, optical, electric and magnetic materials, chemical sensing, catalysis, and biomedicine.

Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as coordination polymers, represent an interesting type of solid crystalline materials that can be straightforwardly self-assembled through the coordination of metal ions/clusters with organic linkers. Owing to the modular nature and mild conditions of MOF synthesis, the porosities of MOF materials can be systematically tuned by judicious selection of molecular building blocks, and a variety of functional sites/groups can be introduced into metal ions/clusters, organic linkers, or pore spaces through pre-designing or post-synthetic approaches. These unique advantages enable MOFs to be used as a highly versatile and tunable platform for exploring multifunctional MOF materials. Here, the bright potential of MOF materials as emerging multifunctional materials is highlighted in some of the most important applications for gas storage and separation, optical, electric and magnetic materials, chemical sensing, catalysis, and biomedicine.
## 1. Introduction
The emergence of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) is one of the most important events in chemistry and materials science communities over the past two decades.[1–4] Before the coin of the term “metal-organic frameworks” by Yaghi in 1995, organicinorganic hybrid materials and coordination polymers have been developed separately from the materials science and chemistry perspectives. The term MOF can differentiate the terms organic-inorganic hybrid materials and coordination polymers from one another other by highlighting the framework nature of such new materials, which mimic the porous nature of zeolite materials.[5,6] The establishment of permanent porosities was indeed realized during 1997–1999 for the very first examples of MOFs, which significantly facilitated the development of this emerging research field within the chemistry and materials science communities.[7–10] MOFs can be straightforwardly self-assembled through the coordination of metal ions/metal clusters with organic linkers. Because of their crystalline nature, they can be readily characterized by single and/or powder X-ray/neutron/synchrotron diffraction studies, which is very important in order to rationalize their structureproperty relationship.[11] From the point of view of structure and material design, MOFs are unique materials and have the following specific features: (i) Rational design: although the usage of this term is debatable, particularly for those chemists who are not familiar with this field, it is definitely true that some of MOFs can be rationally designed with predictable structures/ topologies and porosities.[12–14] This is because the structures of the resulting MOFs are simply based on the geometries of metal clusters and shapes of organic linkers. The rational design principle has been specifically and widely demonstrated in those MOFs having $\mathrm{{M}}_{2}\mathrm{{(COO)}_{4}}$ $(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) of square planar geometry and $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}(C O O)_{6}}}$ of octahedral geometry.[15–18] For example, a number of isoreticular MOFs of general formula $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}[\mathrm{R}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}]_{3}$ have been realized (Figure 1a);[15] while the self-assembly of a $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ secondary building unit with a variety of organic linkers has led to a large number of MOFs of different topologies and pore structures (pore sizes and curvatures) (Figure 1b).[16–18] Such a building-up and rational design principle is very important in order to develop MOF materials whose porosities can be systematically tuned for their multifunctional properties. (ii) Functional sites: the functions of MOF materials can not only be attributed to their pores, but can also be generated from the pore surfaces and nodes.[19–22] For example, the usage of organic linkers with specific sites such as pyridyl and amine sites can readily introduce these sites into the pore surfaces of the resulting MOFs for their specific recognitions of small molecules and thus for their diverse applications.[19] Furthermore, sometimes, multi­ple functional sites can be readily immobilized onto the pore surfaces through the multivariate MOF approach.[23] Functional sites can also be introduced through a post-synthetic approach.[24,25] The establishment of permanent porosities of a few mixed-metal-organic frameworks (M’MOFs) has opened the potential to systematically immobilize functional metal sites into pore surfaces through a metalloligand approach.[26,27] In some circumstances, metal sites within metal cluster nodes can be also accessible to guest molecules and thus can be utilized to develop their functional properties. Broadly speaking, MOF materials can inherit the functions of both metal ions/clusters and organic linkers, so we can also make use of the electronic, magnetic and optical properties of such inorganic and organic building units to develop multifunctional materials.[28–31] (iii) Pore confinement: because we can make use of the pore spaces of MOFs for their specific confinement and inclusion of diverse species such as nanoparticles, metal complexes, organic dyes,[32,33] we can further explore multifunctional MOF materials beyond the traditional organic/inorganic hybrid materials (Figure 2). Here, we highlighted the recent progresses on some of the most important applications of MOF materials for gas storage and separation, optical, electric and magnetic materials, chemical sensing, catalysis, and biomedicine.
## 2. MOFs for Gas Storage and Separation
MOFs have been first and extensively pursued for gas storage and separation. This is understandable because MOFs can have extremely high porosities (MOFs have the record BET surface area over $7000\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and spaces to take up gas molecules, along with tunable pore sizes and functional sites on the pore surfaces to maximize their sieving effects and to differentiate their interactions with gas molecules. Herein we summarize the current status of porous MOFs for gas storage and separation.
