## 2.1. Gas Storage
The potential of MOFs for gas storage has been foreseen right after the discovery of their permanent porosities. Kitagawa and Yaghi independently realized the first two examples of MOFs for methane storage.[6,13] President Bush’s DOE hydrogen initiative program in 2002 not only significantly promoted the research of MOFs for hydrogen storage, but also helped to advance MOF research to a very prosperous stage.
Since the discovery of the first MOF for hydrogen storage in 2003 by Yaghi (for MOF-5),[34,35] hundreds of porous MOFs have been developed as hydrogen storage materials.[36,37] Because MOFs can have very extremely high porosities, while the gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities at 77 K and high pressure are basically proportional to their pore volumes and/or surface areas, MOFs still hold the record total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gravimetric uptake of $17.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar and $77~\mathrm{K}$ for (MOF-210).[38] This value is higher than those of carbon-based adsorbents (e.g., COFs, carbons, and zeolites),[39] and comparable to other promising $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage materials (e.g., metal hydrides and chemical hydrides).[40,41] However, it is an almost formidable challenge to target MOF materials for high hydrogen storage at higher operating temperatures. In fact, no MOFs has met the DOE $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage targets of an energy capacity on a mass basis of $1.8\mathrm{kWh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ system (equivalent to $5.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake) and on a volume basis of $1.3\mathrm{\kWh\L^{-1}}$ system $(40~\mathrm{g/L})$ below 100 bar under an operating temperature of higher than $-40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (current hydrogen storage technologies operate under relatively high
