MOFs for methane storage are very promising and feasible. The interactions between MOFs and methane are moderate, so the methane storage can be realized at room temperature (RT) and moderately high pressure.[43–45] The rich natural gas resources for their potential usage in vehicles have motivated
industrial partners to collaborate closely with academic   
scientists to realize some useful MOF materials for practical   
methane storage, particularly after President Obama’s Methane ortunities For Vehicular Energy (MOVE) program in   
2011.[46] Although the pore structures (pore sizes, curvatures   
and pore size distributions) play certain roles for their methane ge, the overall gravimetric methane storage capacities of   
MOFs under high pressure over 60 bar are pretty much detered by their pore volumes and BET surface areas.[47–49] can be well exemplified by MOF-210 and Al-soc-MOF-1 (Table 1). In terms of volumetric methane storage, HKUST-1 shows record high volumetric capacity of $267~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar.[47] The optimized structure and pore space within HKUST-1 are mainly responsible for its high volumetric methane uptake; this explanation was confirmed by Chen et al. on UTSA-20.[51] Another alternative strategy for increasing the volumetric uptake is to introduce functional groups/ sites into the pores of MOFs (Figure 3).[52,53] In this respect, UTSA-76 with pyrimidine groups shows high volumetric uptake of ${\approx}260~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar, as well as the record high working capacity of ${\approx}200~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . The Lewis basic N sites and dynamic freedom of pyrimidine groups are the two main reasons that result in the remarkably increased storage capacities. However, open metal sites show little effect even a negative effect on enhancing the working capacity, as indicated in MOF-74
series.[43] When most scientists still focused on the development of new conventional MOFs with type I gas sorption isotherms for methane storage, Long and co-workers made significant progress on flexible MOFs that exhibit a high, usable $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity of $\mathrm{197~cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .[54] This work may have opened another door to pursue MOF materials for high methane storage.
Currently, one of the most important technologies for methane storage is compressed natural gas (CNG), stored as supercritical fluid at room temperature and 200−300 bar. The new DOE targets of $0.5~\mathrm{g/g}$ and $263~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ correspond to the density of CNG at 250 bar and $298{\mathrm{~K}}.$ In fact, the best volumetric capacity of $267~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , achieved by HKUST-1, has potentially reached the new DOE target if material packing loss is ignored, which corresponds to the storage density of CNG at 255 bar and $298{\mathrm{~K}}.$ . Besides porous MOF materials, many other porous materials, such as zeolites,[55] activated carbons,[56] carbon nanotubes,[57] covalent organic frameworks (COFs),[58] and porous polymer networks (PPNs),[59] have also been explored as adsorbents for methane storage. The comparison of methane storage capacities of some selected MOFs with other porous materials is listed in Table 1. The relatively low surface areas of zeolites together with their extremely hydrophilic properties minimize their capacities for methane storage, typically below $110\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .[57] The methane storage capacities of the best zeolite 5A are only $0.05~\mathrm{g/g}$ and $106~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at RT and 35 bar.[60] Porous carbon materials (mainly activated carbons and carbon nanotubes) are an alternative material as adsorbents for methane storage. A previous review evaluating the performance of carbons for ANG storage has been published by Parkyns et al., where most porous carbon materials show relatively low methane uptakes in the range of $50{-}160~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .[56] Some great progresses have been made on this type of material in recent years.[61,62] Casco et al. recently demonstrated that the methane storage capacities of some high-surface-area carbon materials are comparable to most promising MOFs (Table 1).[61] The state-of-the-art carbon material (LMA738) shows a high working capacity of $174\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , only a little lower than the best MOF UTSA-76. Robust PPN and COF materials are two other types of promising materials that have been exploited as adsorbents for methane storage.[58,59,63] Superior water stability and high BET surface area (up to $6500~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ can be simply designed on these kinds of porous materials.[63,64] For instance, PPN-4, with a BET surface area of $6461~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , shows a high total gravimetric methane uptake of $0.389\ \mathrm{g/g}$ at RT and 55 bar;[59] the value is comparable to the best MOF materials. However, compared to MOFs, the densities of PPNs and COFs are usually very low, making them less favorable in terms of volumetric storage capacities. Overall, state-of-the-art MOF materials still show the best performances compared to other porous materials, no matter if in terms of volumetric/gravimetric storage or working capacities. Future efforts should be focused on further increasing methane storage working capacity to target the DOE’s values, and implementing some highly robust and affordable MOFs for on-vehicle methane storage.
