The concern of global warming has drawn unprecedented public attention to the issue of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission. The use of porous MOFs as adsorbents has been extensively investigated for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture within the past decades.[65] The total gravimetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture capacities of MOFs at high pressure trend to increase as their BET surface areas increase.[66] For example, the gravimetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes at 40 bar and RT are 1072, 1656, 2315 and $2480\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ , respectively, which monotonically increase with BET surface areas (3800, 4500, 6143 and $6240\ m^{2}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ for MOF-5, MOF-177, NU-100 and MOF-210. Actually, MOF210 also represents the highest total gravimetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of about $2870\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ at RT and 50 bar among all reported MOFs so far,[38] higher than that for two very famous porous polymer networks: PPN-4 $(2121\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ and PAF-1 $\cdot1585\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ at 40 bar).[59] To target high volumetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at high pressure, MOFs also need to have balanced porosities and framework densities to maximize the pore-usage efficiency. Due to the balanced porosity and framework density, NU-111 with a BET surface area of $4900\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ shows the highest absolute volumetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $350\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at RT and 30 bar so far,[67] a value higher than most of zeolites and activated carbons (e.g., zeolite 5A, Zeolite 13X, JX101, and BPL).[65,68]
Microporous MOFs have also been investigated for acetylene storage in recent years. Because of its very explosive nature, acetylene storage must be realized at RT and under pressure of $0.2\mathrm{MPa}$ due to safety concerns. It is thus important to immobilize specific binding sites into MOFs for enhanced interactions with acetylene and thus high acetylene storage. Chen and coworkers published a series of works on this topic, where the incorporation of functional sites, such as OMSs, triple bond, and basic pyridyl sites into MOFs are capable of remarkably increasing the acetylene uptake.[69–71] Recently, Yuan et al. also realized a very promising MOF FJI-H8 with both suitable pore spaces and OMSs, showing the highest gravimetric acetylene uptake of $224{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ so far.[18]
These high-purity single components are in high demand for many applications. A large number of MOF materials have been specially explored for these important separations.[74] $\mathrm{~In~}2012$ , Long and Chen’s groups independently realized that Fe-MOF-74 is capable of separation of hydrocarbon mixtures into individual pure components from 99 to $99.5\%$ purity.[77,78] In particular, Fe-MOF-74 has the highest adsorption selectivity (14) for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation among MOF materials, larger than that of the outstanding zeolite NaX (9.2).[78] In 2014, Yang et al. developed a hydroxyl-functionalized Al-MOF (NOTT-300) with high selectivity for unsaturated hydrocarbons.[79] For $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation, NOTT-300 shows the highest selectivity of 48.7, higher than the state-of-the-art porous aromatic framework (PAF-1- ${\mathrm{.SO}}_{3}{\mathrm{Ag}}$ , 26.9) and zeolites (NaEST-10 (13) and NaX (8)).[80] However, for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation, no porous materials except MOFs have been realized so far to significantly differentiate these two gas molecules due to their very similar sizes and electronic structures. Recently, Chen and co-workers reported a dual-functionalized MOF (UTSA-100) for efficient removal of acetylene from ethylene/acetylene mixtures containing $1\%$ acetylene.[81] As shown in Figure 4a, UTSA-100a has narrow windows of $3.3\mathring\mathrm{\mathrm{A}}$ and suitable cages of $4.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ to enforce its high adsorption selectivity (10.72). The immobilized $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups with their preferential interactions with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules also enable UTSA-100a to take up a moderate amount of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Figure 4b,c). The balanced high separation selectivity and moderate $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake make UTSA-100a superior to other promising MOFs (MOF-74 series and NOTT-300), exhibiting highly efficient removal of acetylene from ethylene/acetylene mixtures.
## 2.2. Gas Separation
Research efforts have been dedicated not only to the development of MOFs for gas storage applications, but also to MOFs for gas separation. Recently, several thematic reviews have highlighted the rapid developments in the design and synthesis of MOF materials for the diverse gas separations.[72,73] Quite a number of important and challenging separations, such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation, light hydrocarbon separation, $\mathrm{~O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation, $\mathrm{CO}/\Nu_{2}$ separation etc., have been achieved by making use of the emerging MOFs as adsorbent materials.
The separation of light hydrocarbons $\left(\mathsf{C}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\right)$ into single components is a very important industrial process because all of them are very important energy resources or raw chemicals in the petrochemical industry.
Carbon dioxide, as a major greenhouse gas, has attracted more and more attention due to its sharply increased emissions into the atmosphere causing global climate change. Much attention is being paid to develop feasible strategies to effectively reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions. The state-of-the-art capture technologies are mainly based on liquid or solid adsorbents. At present, the main industrial technology is to use liquid amine-based absorbents to chemically adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue gas at nearly ambient temperature (mostly $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ).[82] However, this process suffers from the drawbacks of the corrosive nature of the solvents, fouling of the process equipment, and high energy cost for solvent regeneration.[83] To overcome the above problems, solid-supported amines based on certain support materials (such as oxide supports) as chemisorbents are intensively investigated and show good performances on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation.[84,85] Other alternative solid adsorbents are these physisorbents that encompass porous MOFs,[22] zeolites,[86,87] carbons,[88] silica,[89] and porous organic polymers (PNNs and COFs).[90–93] Compared with chemisorptive amines for complete $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on these materials is “physical” and weak, making these adsorbents relatively low in selectivity. Therefore, physisorptive selectivity is an important parameter for evaluating these solid adsorbents’ performance. Recent studies show that the cost for capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be significantly reduced if the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity is enhanced above 150 for an adsorbent with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of 4 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[94] To date, the best performing zeolite for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture is NaKA with a $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ content of $9.9~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , showing the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity over 1100 with a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity (4 mmol $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\bar{\Phi}}}\end{array}$ ).[87] Aminated $\mathsf{P P N-6-C H_{2}D E T A}$ exhibits the largest selectivity of 442 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ among all porous organic polymers reported so far.[91] These materials are very remarkable, demonstrating great potential for practical application in post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture technology.
In recent years, tremendous efforts have also been dedicated to the development of MOF-based $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbents.[22,65] In this hot topic, incorporation of functional sites and tuning of pore sizes/sharps in MOFs are very promising strategies to effectively improve their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and separation capacities.[95–103] For example, Mg-MOF-74 with extremely high density of open $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ sites, presents the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity $(37.9\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ at ambient conditions.[95] By control of pore sizes in SIFSIX series, Zaworotko et al. realized a very promising MOF SIFSIX-3-Zn with $3.84\times3.84\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ pore channels, showing the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity (1700) for any MOF materials reported so far.[96] This value is superior to other porous materials and comparable to those chemisorptive solid amine materials.[85,89] To further decrease the pore size to $3.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , the resultant SIFSIX-3-Cu offers enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and selectivity for trace and low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration removal.[97] Xiang et al. also discovered that UTSA-16, with immobilized terminal water molecules and suitable pore/cage space, exhibits extraordinarily high separation selectivity (314.7) and uptake capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture at ambient conditions.[98]
Long and co-workers demonstrated that the alkylamino-functionalization within porous MOFs though post-synthetic modification can improve low-pressure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption selectivity and capacity.[101] Especially, functionalization of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc)
$\mathrm{[M=Mg}$ , Mn, Fe, Co, $Z\mathrm{n}$ ; dobpdc4 $^-=4,4^{\prime}$ -dioxidobiphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ dicarboxylate) with $\mathrm{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylethylenediamine (mmen) led to “phase-change” adsorbents exhibiting unusual step-shaped $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms.[102] The origin of the sharp adsorption step was found to be an unprecedented cooperative process in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules insert into metal-amine bonds, inducing a reorganization of the amines into well-ordered chains of ammonium carbamate (Figure 5a–f). As a result, large $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation capacities can be fulfilled with small temperature swings (Figure 5g–h), providing a new mechanistic framework that is superior to other solid or liquid sorbents for the efficient capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
Separation of pentane or hexane isomers has become a very important process pertaining to the gas, oil, and fuel industries. A high octane number (ON) for a gasoline fuel is very important and directly correlated to the performance of an engine. Given the fact that branched isomers have a higher ON value, separation of linear paraffins (such as n-pentane, n-hexane) from branched isomers can efficiently boost the ON value in gasoline. Conventional porous adsorbents, such as zeolites and activated carbons, have already been widely used as separation adsorbents.[104,105] Current industrial refining processes for hexane blends by using zeolites can generate a final RON of nearly 83.[104] Recently, the potential utility of porous MOFs for separation of pentane or hexane isomers has been explored by several independent groups.[106–108] The state-of-the-art MOF material has achieved a high RON greater than 90. The first example of kinetic separation of hexane isomers by fixed-bed adsorption with $[\mathrm{Zn(bdc)(dabco)_{0.5}}]$ $[\mathrm{bdc}^{-}=1,4]$ -benzenedicarboxylate; dabco $=1,4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) was reported by Chen and workers.[106] This material shows extraordinary separation selectivity to separate branched isomers from linear nHEX. In 2013, Long and co-workers developed a new framework $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathsf{b d p})_{3}$ with triangular channels for the separation of hexane isomers.[107] The bed packed with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathsf{b d p})_{3}$ is able to separate an equimolar mixture of all five hexane isomers into three fractions: the dibranched isomers, the monobranched isomers and linear n-hexane, attributed to their different van der Waals interactions with the surface of triangular channels. During the beginning of the breakthrough experiment, the RON of the eluted mixture rises to greater than 90, notably higher than the value of 83 that is typical for industrially refined hexane blends. Recently, Eddaoudi and co-workers developed a novel rare-earth metal (RE) fcu-MOF with tunable aperture sizes for practical steric adsorptive separation of branched paraffins from normal ones (Figure 6).[108]
After great research effects on porous MOFs as adsorbents, some of other gas separations including $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ aliphatic compounds, $\mathrm{CO}/\Nu_{2}.$ and harmful gases have also been achieved in the last decades. Pure $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ has been receiving considerable attention for its potential utility in improving the efficiency of several chemical processes such as natural gas combustion, coal gasification, sewage treatment, and welding. The C6−C10 isomeric compounds are important industrial feedstock chemicals. CO has become an increasingly important chemical for the synthesis of a variety of chemical commodities, such as many monomers and polymers, alcohols, and acetic acid. The sharply increasing harmful gases, such as CO, $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , and fluorocarbons, cause serious harm to the health of human beings.
Therefore, the separations or removals of these gases or compounds are very important for the industry and our environment. Long and co-workers realized the use of Cr-HKUST-1 and MOF-74 series for efficient $\mathrm{~O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation.[109,110] The best $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity factor can reach 22, higher than that of cobalt(II) complexes appended to a silica support (ca. 4) but lower than the state-of-the-art ionic liquids membrane with a selectivity of 60.[111,112] Chen et al. reported the first example of using a gas chromatography (GC) column packed with single crystals of MOF-508 to separate aliphatic isomers.[113] Zhang et al. revealed that MAF-6 is a promising candidate for GC separation of C6−C10 linear alkanes and C6 alkane isomers.[114] By introducing open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions into a soft nanoporous MOF, Kitagawa and co-workers achieved unprecedented highly effective trapping of CO from a gas mixture with $\Nu_{2}$ .[115] A high $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation selectivity of 216 can be achieved on $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ framework reported by Long et al., suggesting its bright potential as a solid adsorbent to replace current cryogenic distillation technologies.[116] Recently, some MOFs have also displayed excellent performances on adsorption and separation of harmful gases, such as fluorocarbons, chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , NO, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , CO, $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ and so on.[117–119]
As membrane techniques for gas separation have some inherent advantages such as high energy efficiency, low cost, easy operation, and recyclability, MOF materials have also been widely fabricated into thin membranes for gas separation.[31] For example, Zhu and co-workers successfully prepared a new HKUST-1 membrane on oxidized copper nets. This HKUST-1 membrane shows good selectivities for $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separations.[120] Caro and co-workers explored the first
ZIF membrane based on the framework ZIF-8. Due to the narrow size of the six-membered ring pores $(\approx3.4\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , this membrane exhibits good performance on $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity in a 1:1 $\mathrm{|\mathrm{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture $_{(\approx11.2)}$ .[121] A similar molecular sieving effect for a high degree of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation from other gases including $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was also observed on ZIF-7, ZIF-22, and ZIF-90.[122,123] The gas-separation performances of these MOF membranes are superior to zeolite membranes.[31] For example, the ZIF-90 membrane shows a high $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ selectivity of 62.5, which is significantly higher than the promising zeolite membranes, including zeolite X (4.5)[124] and silicate-1 (1.84).[125]
As discussed above, MOFs are certainly very promising materials for a wide range of gas storage and separation applications. However, gas storage and separation in practical applications involves more variables than most laboratory-scale evaluations. In the case of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation from flue gases, the partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is very low, and the stream has a high temperature and contains water and other gases that can potentially poison the MOF binding sites. In this context, thermal, water, and framework stability are very crucial to the practical applications. Recently, several water-stable zirconium MOFs have been developed with ultrahigh surface areas up to $6550~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[126] In addition, the incorporation of hydrophobic linkers or polymers into MOFs is able to significantly improve their water stability.[103,127,128] These new findings are very encouraging, rendering MOF materials attractive for gas storage and separation in practical applications. We believe that with continued close collaboration between chemists, chemical engineers, and computer modeling scientists, some of these unique MOFs will eventually be implemented in practical industrial applications in the near future.
## 3. MOFs for Optical Materials
To incorporate luminescent components into MOFs, this emerging type of organic-inorganic hybrid materials are certainly very promising as luminescent solid materials.[129,130] The richness of metal ions/clusters and a large number of organic compounds as linkers have endowed great promise to not only designing an enormous amount of new luminescent MOF materials but also to purposefully tune their luminescence properties.[129] Benefiting from their high porosities, MOF materials also process unique advantages to serve as rigid/ flexible hosts for the encapsulation of guest luminescent species such as lanthanide ions,[131] fluorescent dyes,[132] quantum dots,[133] and light-emissive building blocks.[134] As a result, hundreds of luminescent MOFs have been developed and widely examined in recent years for their potential applications in light-emitting devices for white light and near-infrared light emitting, nonlinear optics, two-photon pumped lasing, and 3D patterning and data storage.
## 3.1. White Light-Emitting Devices
Solid-state light-emitting-diode (LED) materials, especially white light-emitting diodes (WLEDs), have become a subject of intense research because of their potential applications in lighting and displays. Common approaches to fabricate WLEDs mainly include: (a) a single white-emitting phosphor on a UV chip, in which multiple components that emit either three primary colors (red, green, and blue); (b) a yellow-emitting phosphor on a blue LED chip. High-quality white-light illumination requires a source with the Commission International de l’Eclairage (CIE) coordinates (0.333; 0.333), with correlated color temperature (CCT) between 2500 and $6500~\mathrm{K},$ and color rendering index (CRI) above 80. Among these standards, the CRI is a very important parameter for WELDs, and a value of 80 is basic requirement for human eye-friendly applications. Current commercially available white LEDs are based on blue InGaN LEDs that excite a yellow-emitting YAG: $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ phosphor. However, due to the deficiency of the red emission, this commercial $\mathrm{YAG}\mathrm{:Ce}^{3+}$ -based white LED produces a cold white light that exhibits an unsatisfactory high CCT of 7750 K and low CRI of 64.[135] Such a low CRI can be improved to as high as 85 by co-doping of red emitting rare-earth ions such as $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Pr}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ into $\mathrm{YAG}{:}\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ , or by mixing with red emitting phosphors or quantum dots (QDs).[136,137] In addition, tremendous efforts have also been dedicated to search for single white phosphor materials or better yellow-emitting phosphors for use in the WLEDs, including hybrid inorganic materials,[136] organic compounds/polymers,[138,139] organic−inorganic perovskite,[140]
## www.MaterialsViews.com
and metal complexes,[141] some of which show the extremely high CRI values over 90. For example, the perovskite (N-MPDA) $\mathrm{[PbBr_{4}]}$ (N-MPDA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $\mathsf{N}^{1}$ -methylpropane-1,3-diammonium) reported by Dohner et al. has a CRI of 85,[140] and an excellent CRI of 98 is obtained from the inorganic material $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Ca}_{6\mathrm{-x-y}}\mathrm{Mg}_{\mathrm{x-z}}(\mathrm{P-}}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{O_{4})_{4};E u_{y}^{\ 2+},M n_{z}^{\ 2+}}$ mixed with $\mathrm{Ca_{2}M g S i_{2}O_{7}{:}E u^{2+}}$ on a UV-LED.[142] Attributed to the high porosity, structural diversity, and tunable luminescence, MOF materials possess some unique advantages for the development of white light-emitting materials. In recent years, a large number of luminescent MOFs have been reported for this purpose. The CRI value for the best MOF can reach as high as 96,[143] which is comparable to other promising materials.
Lanthanide MOFs (Ln-MOFs) have been widely employed to fulfill highly pure white light emission; because the emission color of Ln-MOFs can be easily modulated both by doping different $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ types/concentrations and by physical para­ meters (excitation wavelength and temperature).[144–146] For example, by doping minute quantities of red-emitting $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ into the host framework $\mathrm{[La(mbdc)(STP)]}$ $[{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{mbdc}}=$ isophthalic acid, NaSTP $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ sodium 4- $(2,2^{\prime};6^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime}$ -terpyridin- $\cdot4^{\prime}$ -yl)benzenesulfonate)), Wu and co-workers achieved the nearly pure white-light emission with the CIE coordinate of (0.364, 0.364), a CCT of $4411\ \mathrm{K},$ and a high CRI of 94.[145] With rational combination of the blue color from pda ligand ( $\mathrm{\PDA=}$ pyridine2,6-dicarboxylate), green color from $\mathrm{T}\bar{\mathsf{b}}^{3+}$ and red color from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , the white-light emission was observed in two doped ZJU$1{:}\mathrm{T}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ systems with the corresponding CIE coordinates of (0.3269, 0.3123) and (0.3109, 0.3332), where the quantum yield can reach $6.8\%$ .[146]
Although doping the framework structures by rare metals at various concentrations indeed has a great opportunity to achieve white light emission, the potential supply risks and sharply raised prices have pushed the search for alternative RE-free phosphors to a level of high importance. Moreover, the quantum yields of Ln-MOFs are quite low due to the less energy transfer from the ligand to lanthanide ions. Since the first RE-free, white-emitting MOF material was realized by Guo and co-workers in 2009,[147] several subsequent studies on various RE-free MOFs have been developed to target white light emitters with high quantum yields.[148–151]
In 2013, Li and co-workers illustrated a new strategy to achieve high quantum yield white light by encapsulating a yellow-emitting $\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complex into the cavity of a blueemitting MOF.[134] This mesoporous anionic MOF contains two types of large cages with the dimensions of 2 and $3\mathrm{nm}$ . The complex $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ $({\mathrm{Hppy}}\ =\ 2$ -phenylpyridine, $\mathsf{b p y}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine), emitting yellow light at $570~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , was successfully loaded into the pores to form $[\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{bpy)}]^{+}@\mathrm{MOF}$ at various concentrations. The resultant composite emitter exhibits two emission maxima at 425 and $530~\mathrm{nm}$ , and the intensity of the emission at $530\mathrm{nm}$ decreases monotonically as the $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ amount decreases. At an $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ concentration of $3.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , high-quality white light was obtained with CIE coordinates of (0.31, 0.33), a high CRI value of 80, and moderate CCT value of 5900 K. The quantum yield was measured to be $20.4\%$ , among the highest of white-light-emitting MOFs reported so far.
Using a similar strategy, Qian and co-workers introduced both the red-light emitting dye 4-(p-dimethylaminostyryl)-1-methylpyridinium (DSM) and the green-light emitting dye acriflavine (AF) into a blue-emitting anionic MOF $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})_{3}[I n_{3}(b t b)_{4}]}$ · $12\mathrm{DMF}\cdot22\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (ZJU-28, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btb}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -benzene-1,3,5-triyl-tribenzoic acid) to yield the ZJU-28⊃DSM/AF composites.[149] By careful adjustment of the relative concentration of DSM and AF, ZJU$28{\supset}\mathrm{DSM/AF}$ $(0.02\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ DSM, $0.06~\mathrm{wt\%}$ AF) showed a white light emission with CIE coordinates of (0.34, 0.32), high CRI value of 91, moderate CCT value of $5327~\mathrm{K},$ and significantly enhanced fluorescence quantum efficiency $(17.4\%)$ . In addition, a high CRI of 93 was also achieved by Nenoff and co-workers though co-doping of $10\%$ $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ into a novel MOF (SMOF-1).[150]
The combination of a yellow-emitting phosphor on a blue LED chip is another common approach to generate white light emission. In this regard, Li and co-workers utilized a highly emissive fluorophore ligand tppe (1,1,2,2-tetrakis(4-(pyridin4-yl)phenyl)ethane), to construct a new RE-free yellow phosphor made of MOF, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{6}(\mathrm{btc})_{4}(\mathrm{tppe})_{2}(\mathrm{sol})_{2}]$ $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}\ =$ 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate; Figure $\mathsf{J a-c})$ .[151] When excited by a blue light source, this material emits bright yellow light at $540\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a high quantum yield of $90.7\%$ . The emission properties are remarkably similar with a commercial yellow phosphor, cerium-doped yttrium aluminum garnet $(\mathrm{YAG};\mathrm{Ce}^{3+})$ . When this material was assembled in a prototype WLED device with a $5\ \mathrm{mm}\ 455{-}460\ \mathrm{nm}$ blue LED bulb, bright white light can be generated with luminous efficacy of $47.4\mathrm{lm/W}$ (Figure 7d), above the requirement of commercial LED lamps $(40\ln/\mathrm{W})$ . In combination with its high moisture and thermal stability and high quantum yield, these features make it a promising material for use in phosphor conversion WLEDs.
## 3.2. Near-Infrared (NIR) Emitting
NIR light-emitting devices have attracted much attention due to their potential applications in broad fields of identification in military action, night-vision illumination sources, consumer electronics, spectroscopic analysis, and bio-imaging. Lanthanide ions such as $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ ( $1530~\mathrm{nm})$ , ${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ ( $\left[1060\ \mathrm{nm}\right]$ ), and ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ $(980\ \mathrm{nm})$ can be readily immobilized into MOFs for preparing the NIR luminescent materials. To date, examples of NIR lightemitting MOFs are scarcely developed compared to visible emitting MOFs, mainly attributed to the poor sensitive effect of most ligands and quite small energy gap between the ground and excited electronic states that is easily matched by $\mathrm{C-H}$ , $_{\mathrm{O-H}}$ , or $_\mathrm{N-H}$ vibration.[129] Therefore, the reported NIR emitting MOFs usually display very low quantum yields compared with the well-known lanthanide-doped nanoparticles and heteronuclear lanthanide complexes.[152,153]
To reduce/eliminate such quenching effects and improve the quantum yields, some strategies have been explored to shield the ion excited levels to high nonradiative transition probability by O−H, C−H, and N−H oscillators. Chen and co-workers realized that the incorporation of fluorinated organic linkers within the Ln-MOFs can effectively enhance the NIR luminescence intensities and quantum yields.[154] Mazzanti and co-workers demonstrated that removal of water from Ln-MOFs to form dehydrated framework can reduce/eliminate the quenching effects of $_{\mathrm{O-H}}$ oscillators, leading to the remarkably enhanced NIR luminescence with quantum yield of $0.18\%$ .[155] In addition, incorporation of the d-block chromophores into Ln-MOFs, whose excitation domain is located at the visible region, can also be used to improve the NIR emission based on the $\mathrm{d{\rightarrow}f}$ energy transfer from transition ions to lanthanide ions.[144]
Rosi et al. created a new barcoded system based on mixed MOFs that contain multiple NIR-emitting lanthanides.[156] They prepared a family of isostructural mixed Ln-MOFs $[\mathrm{Er_{x}Y b_{1-x}}\mathrm{.PVDC-1}]$ $(\mathrm{x}=0.32$ , 0.58, 0.70, and 0.81; $\mathrm{\cal{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\pvdc}= $ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -[(2,5-dimethoxy-1,4-phenylene)di-2,1-ethenediyl]bis-benzoic acid) by varying the amounts of ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ ions within a Ln-MOF (Yb-PVDC-1) (Figure 8a). These mixed Ln-MOFs show sharp NIR-emitting signals from both $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ ( $\cdot980~\mathrm{nm})$ and $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ ( $1530~\mathrm{nm})$ upon excitation at $490~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 8b,c). The relative intensity of the ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ NIR emissions are linearly related to their ratio in Yb-PVDC-1, which can be finely tuned by controlling these two lanthanide ions (Figure 8d). These intrinsic features show potential for color-coded barcode readouts. The number and diversity of barcodes can be increased by employing a larger number of $\mathrm{{Ln/Ln}}$ ratios or by incorporating additional lanthanide ions into the framework, as demonstrated by $\mathrm{Nd}_{0.09}\mathrm{Er}_{0.55}\mathrm{Yb}_{0.36}$ -PVDC-1 consisting of NIR signals from three lanthanide ions.
Two NIR luminescent Ln-MOFs have also been reported for up-conversion properties, which can convert lower energy NIR light to higher-energy visible light through multiphoton processes.[157,158] For example, Weber et al. reported five isostructural 2D Ln-MOFs containing ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $[(\mathrm{Tb}_{\mathrm{1-x}}\mathrm{Yb}_{\mathrm{x}})(\mathrm{DPA})$ (HDPA)] $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DPA}=}}\end{array}$ pyridine 2,6-dicarboxylic acid). The excitation of the $\mathrm{Yb^{3+2}F}_{7/2}\rightarrow{^{2}\mathrm{F}}_{5/2}$ transition $(980~\mathrm{nm})$ led to visible up-conversion emission of $\mathrm{\ddot{Tb}^{3+5}D_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}F_{J}}$ transitions. As the
## www.MaterialsViews.com
$\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion has no energy levels in the NIR region to absorb radiation, the observed $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ emissions are described as a two photon up-conversion excitation mechanism.
## 3.3. Nonlinear Optics (NLO) Materials
NLO materials, as frequency conversion devices, have widespread applications in lasing devices and optical communication technologies. One of the most common behaviors of NLO properties is second-harmonic generation (SHG), in which two photons are combined to form a new one with twice the frequency. Commonly, an SHG-active material must lack a center of symmetry. Noncentrosymmetric MOFs can be readily designed and synthesized to provide SHG properties by making use of the rational choice of metal nodes and bridging ligands.[159] The state-of-the-art MOF material shows the remarkable SHG intensity of $1000\times\alpha$ quartz,[160] which is higher than the technologically important lithium niobate $({\mathrm{LiNb}}0_{3}$ , $600~\times~\alpha$ -quartz) and other inorganic compounds including metal borates,[161] metal iodates,[162] and metal oxides.[163] For comparison, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{IO}_{3})_{6}$ synthesized by Chang et al. shows the best SHG efficiency of $\approx500\times\alpha$ -quartz among metal iodates.[164] Lin and co-workers first reported the use of tetrahedral metal centers $(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+},\mathrm{Cd}^{2+})$ and unsymmetrical linear bridging ligands (such as $p$ -pyridinecarboxylate and its derivatives) for construction of a series of noncentrosymmetric MOFs with the diamondoid topology.[165,166] The unsymmetrical bridging ligands are believed to break the center of symmetry in MOFs, thus leading to the impressive SHG activities up to $1000\times\alpha$ quartz, larger than technologically important lithium ${\mathrm{LiNb}}O_{3}$ . As the $p$ -pyridinecarboxylate ligand becomes longer, the void space within a single network gets larger to result in a higher degree of interpenetration.[167] An even-number-fold interpenetration can potentially lead to centrosymmetric MOF, while an odd-number-fold will necessarily crystallize in a noncentrosymmetric space group. Thus, the SHG activity can be controlled by a simple choice of bridging ligands with appropriate lengths.
A similar symmetry strategy was also adopted by other research groups to synthesize numerous noncentrosymmetric MOFs with SHG activities. For example, Kang et al. employed isonicotinate ligands with $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ nodes to prepare an interpenetrated 3D framework in the noncentrosymmetric space group Pca21 and demonstrated its SHG activity, comparable to that of KDP $\mathrm{(KH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\mathrm{)}$ .[168] Wang et al. synthesized two Cd MOFs with benzoimidazoles, which crystallize in a noncentrosymmetric 3D sqp-net for strong SHG efficiencies of 3 times that of KDP.[169]
Unlike the above noncentrosymmetric MOFs, Qian, Chen and co-workers developed a new strategy to construct highly SHG-active MOF materials by incorporation of the ordered organic dipolar chromophores into the anionic framework ZJU-28.[170] As shown in Figure 9a, four different pyridinium hemicyanine cationic chromophores with a systematically tuned alkyl chain length (DPASM, DPASB, DPASN, and DPASD) can be easily incorporated into the framework via ion exchange. The inclusion of these hemicyanine chromophores tunes the ZJU-28 material from NLO inactive to NLO active materials. The SHG intensities of these materials are heavily dependent on the included hemicyanine chromophore guest ions; the longer the terminal alkyl chain, the stronger the SHG intensity of the resulting material (Figure 9b). As a result, ZJU$28\supset$ DPASD shows the highest SHG intensity of $18.3\times\alpha$ quartz because of its longest alkyl chain.
## 3.4. Two-Photon-Pumped Lasing
Two-photon-excited fluorescence and two-photon-pumped (TPP) lasing are very important because of their diverse applications in optical data storage, optical limiting, biological imaging and photodynamic therapy. TPP lasing does not require stringent phase-matching, which is certainly superior to SHG-based frequency up-conversion. Although TPP lasing behaviors have been realized in several organic dyes solutions, solid-state TPP lasers are very difficult to realize in the solid state because of the aggregation-caused quenching.
Recently, by encapsulating suitable dyes into the pores of MOFs, two-photon imaging[171] and lasing[132] have been well documented in the solid state. Yu et al. reported the first TPP micro-laser by encapsulating the cationic dye (4-[p-(dimethylamino)styryl]-1-methylpyridinium (DMASM)) into the anionic bio-MOF-1 (Figure 10a).[132] Due to encapsulation of DMASM into the pores, the resulting bio-MOF$1{\supset}$ DMASM composite exhibits a much stronger emission at $610\ \mathrm{nm}$ , with a quantum efficiency of $25.87\%$ , than that of the DMASM solution and powder (Figure 10b–e), attributable to the pore confinement of DMASM molecules within bio-MOF-1. The large TPA cross-section of DMASM dyes and significantly enhanced luminescent efficiency enable bio-MOF1⊃DMASM to generate significant two-photon excited fluorescence. As shown in Figure 10f, TPP lasing around $640~\mathrm{nm}$ was observed in the bio-MOF- $1=$ DMASM when pumped with a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ pulse laser at room temperature. The lasing intensities can be gradually enhanced by the increased pump energy with a threshold of $0.148~\mathrm{m}]$ J, attributed to a Fabry–Perot cavity mechanism. This study suggests a new strategy for design of highly efficient TPP lasers based on MOFs, thus opening a new way for the future creation of solid-state photonic materials and devices. During the revision of this manuscript, He et al. used the same strategy to realize the first polarized three-photonpumped microcavity laser in a single MOF crystal.[172]
## 3.5. MOFs for 3D Patterning and Data Storage
Qian, Chen and co-workers reported the first example of twophoton responsive MOF (ZJU-56−0.20) by incorporation of a photoactive ligand, (2,5-bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)-1-methylpyridinium hydroxide) into ZJU-56 for 3D two-photon patterning (Figure 11a,b).[173] Due to the photochemical reactions of the photoactive linkers, ZJU-56−0.20 exhibits a significant twophoton excited fluorescence change in response to a $710\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser (Figure 11c). However, the laser at ${900}\mathrm{nm}$ , which cannot initiate photochemical reactions, is able to excite the written areas, generating strong two-photon excited fluorescence. Thus, the fluorescent patterns can be fully written and read through the two-photon processes with the laser at 710 and ${900}~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. This unique two-photon response allows for two-photon patterning and imaging in high-resolution $(1\times1\times5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}^{3};$ 3D structure (Figure 11d–g). Because of the irreversible photochemical process, the fluorescent pattern is very stable and does not show much decay even after 30 days, making it to be a useful material for sensor arrays and data storage media.
Very recently, Hong et al. realized the solid-state multiphoton-excited photoluminescence (MEPL) in a single MOF.[174] This material exhibits the impressive two-, three-, and four-photon-excited photoluminescence under the excitation at 800, 1200 and $1500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Their results will further enable MOFs to be efficient luminescent materials for their applications in data storage media.
## 4. MOFs for Conductive Materials
## 4.1. Electrical Conductivity
Research on conductive MOFs that require electron transport or conductivity in combination with permanent porosity, to certain extent, still lag behind those hot topics on functional MOFs for gas storage, separation, luminescence, chemical sensing, and heterogeneous catalysis.[175,176] This is understandable; because the vast majority of porous MOFs are typically electrical insulators that rarely provide a good conjugation pathway for charge transport. Such low electrical conductivity of porous MOFs certainly hampers their further utility in a number of desirable applications, such as porous electrodes and electrocatalysts as well as key components in fuel cells, capacitors, sensors etc. Despite a grand challenge, tremendous progresses still have been made in the past six years for the construction of a variety of 2D and 3D conductive MOFs with high charge mobility and/or electrical conductivity. To data, the state-of-the-art $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BHT})_{2}}$ thin film prepared by Zhu and co-workers shows the record high conductivity of $1580\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ (four-probe) at room temperature for any reported MOFs so far.[177] This value is very remarkable, not only comparable to that of the commercial graphite but also higher than the best conjugated polymer nanofibers,[178] mesoporous carbons,[179] COFs,[180] and 1D nanoribbons.[181] As a comparison, the 1D $\mathrm{[Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{dta})_{4}\mathrm{I]_{n}}$ (dta $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dithioacetato) nano­ ribbons show an intrinsic conductivity over $10^{4}\mathrm{~S~m~}^{-1}$ .[181] In this section, we mainly introduce the reported design strategies that have been employed for the construction of conductive MOF materials, and summarize the recent advances on this research area.
Judicious choice of electroactive metal and organic components can lead to crystalline MOF materials with promising conductivity and/or charge mobility properties. One of the promising strategies is to use sulfur-based bridging ligands that form strong interactions with metal ions to form conductive MOFs. In 2009, Kitagawa and co-workers published the first conductive MOF $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ constructed from $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ and pdt (pdt $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2,3$ -pyrazinedithiolate) linkers through both $\operatorname{Cu-}S$ and $\mathrm{Cu-N}$ bonds, exhibiting a relatively high electrical conductivity of $6\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at 300 K.[182] Long et al. found that the conductivity of the isostructural $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ reaches $1\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ after partial oxidation by $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ vapor.[183] Using the sulfur atoms to replace oxygen atoms in metal−oxygen chains of the existing MOFs, Dinc˘a and co-workers reported a conductive MOF $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})$ containing the infinite one-dimensional $\mathrm{Mn}{-}S$ chains and high porosity, exhibiting an excellent charge mobility of $0.01\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{\bar{V}}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ .[184]
Stacking of electroactive molecules, such as TTF-TCNQ $\mathrm{TTF}=$ tetrathiafulvalene, $\mathrm{TCNQ}=$ tetracyanoquiondimethane), has been exploited as a very promising strategy to construct porous and conductive 3D MOFs.[185–188] With an expanded TTF-TC ligand as a linker, Dincˇa et al. realized the first example of porous conductive MOF, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{(TTFTB)}$ .[186] This framework contains columnar stacks of TTF molecules with $\approx3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ apart, and benzoate-lined infinite 1D channels of $\approx6$ Å in diameter. The MOF shows a high charge mobility of $0.2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{V}.\mathrm{s}$ , comparable to or higher than most organic conductive polymers. The electrical conductivity can be finely tuned by varying the metal cation employed in the synthesis of TTFTB-based MOFs from $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , Of which $\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{TTFTB})$ with the shortest S…S contact shows the largest conductivity of $2.86\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ .[187] $\mathrm{In}2015$ , Long and co-workers constructed a new MOF by $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and the redox-active $\mathrm{dhbq}^{2-}/{}^{3-}$ ligands $(\mathrm{dhbq}^{2-}/^{3-}=2,5$ -dioxidobenzoquinone/1,2-dioxido-4,5-semiquinone) for a high conductivity of $0.16\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at 298 K.[188]
Recently, a family of planar 2D $\pi$ -conjugated MOFs reminiscent of graphene have been developed, and promoted the conductivity value higher by several orders of magnitude.[189–191] All this kind of MOFs are made from benzene- or triphenylenederived ligands containing ortho-disubstituted N, O, or S donor atoms to coordinate with a variety of metal nodes. For example, using the nitrogen-based electroactive 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaaminotriphenylene (HATP), Dinc˘a and co-workers reported a novel 2D conductive MOF $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ .[189] The bulk pellet and surface film of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ have a high conductivity of 2 and $40\ \mathrm{S}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The efficient 2D conjugation pathways between electroactive organic molecules are believed to result in the impressive electrical properties. Several conductive 2D MOFs based on metal dithiolene have also been reported.[190,191] In 2013, Nishihara and co-workers synthesized a new 2D $\pi$ -conjugated MOF $[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BHT})_{2}]$ by reaction of benzenehexathiol (BHT) and ${\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2}$ . The van der Pauw method on a single microflake of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BHT})_{2}$ showed a very high conductivity of $1.6\times10^{2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ .[190] By replacing the Ni sites with Cu ions, Huang et al. realized that the $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BHT})_{2}}$ thin film displays the record high conductivity of $1580\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ (four-probe) at room-temperature for all the reported MOFs to date.[177]
Another alternative strategy for achieving highly electrical conductivity in MOFs is to introduce redox-active, conjugated guest molecules into the nanopores of MOF materials.[192] MOFs with OMSs indeed offer a large number of coordination positions to immobilize guest redox-active molecules into the pores. This approach was demonstrated by Talin et al. using thin-film devices of the MOF $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ infiltrated with the redox-active molecule 7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinododimethane (TCNQ) (Figure 12a). The activated $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ thin-film device has a very low conductivity $\mathrm{0f\approx10^{-6}~S~c m^{-1}}$ , indicating its insulating nature. After infiltrating this thin-film device into a saturated TCNQ $/\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ solution for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ , the conductivity can be drastically increased to $7\mathrm{{S}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}}$ (Figure 12b). Such increased conductivity mainly arises from the adsorbed TCNQ molecules bridging the neighboring binuclear copper paddlewheels, leading to strong electronic coupling between the dimeric Cu subunits to create a continuous pathway for charge transfer (Figure 12c,d).
## 4.2. Proton Conductivity
The use of MOFs as proton conducting materials for their important applications in fuel cells represents another direction in MOF research which has been highlighted by several previous review articles.[193,194] For this application, the stateof-the-art material is Nafion, as an industry standard polymer electrolyte for fuel cells, whose conductivity can reach in the order of $10^{-1}{-}10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ under a high relative humidity $98\%$ RH).[195] However, the use of Nafion as electrolyte suffers from its low thermal and chemical stability when operating over a wide performing temperature and humidity range. The required performances for a better electrolyte are: high proton conductivity $\mathrm{(>10^{-2}~S~c m^{-1})}$ to facilitate efficient transport of protons between electrodes; good thermal and chemical stability under fuel cell operating conditions. Due to the tunability of structures, functionalities, and excellent stability, MOFs present an intriguing potential for the development of porous proton-conducting materials. Until now, a growing number of proton-conducting MOFs have been explored, and the performance has been tested in two distinct regimes: watermediated proton-conducting MOFs operating below $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and anhydrous proton conductors operating above $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Some of water-stable MOF materials have achieved the high proton conductivities over $10^{-2}~\mathrm{S~cm^{-1}}$ under humidity environments, whose values are comparable to that of Nafion and superior to the best COFs and other porous materials.[179,196,197]
Most proton-conducting MOFs reported in the literature operate under low-temperature conditions, and are aided by the presence of water molecules and hydrogen-bond networks. Any improvement in the water-mediated proton conductivity of MOFs requires the addition of an acid functionality. This finding was first demonstrated by Kitagawa and co-workers in a series of isostructural frameworks, M(OH)(bdc-R) $[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Al}$ Fe; ${\sf R}={\sf H}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , OH, $(\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}]$ , where the proton conductivity can be fine-tuned by the $\mathtt{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of ligand functional groups.[198]
The acidity of the functional groups can be simply tuned by immobilization of other acidic groups, such as carboxylic,[199,200] sulfonic,[201] ammonium,[202] imidazolium,[203] or phosphonic[204,205] acid species, as channel-accessible onto framework linkers to facilitate proton conductivities in MOFs. For example, using a phosphonic acid as a linker, Shimizu and co-workers presented a robust 3D MOF $\mathrm{\small{[La(H_{5}L)(H_{2}O)_{4}]}}$ (PCMOF-5, $\mathrm{L}=1,2,4,5$ -tetrakisphosphonomethylbenzene) containing a large number of free phosphonic acid groups.[204] PCMOF-5 shows a high proton conductivity of $2.5\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ at $98\%$ RH and $335\ \mathrm{K}.$ Yaghi et al. reported a new 3D framework $\mathrm{Fe(THO)\cdotFe(SO_{4})\cdot(D M A)_{3}}$ (Fe-CAT-5; ${\mathrm{THO^{6-}}}=$ triphenylene-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexakis(olate)) with the coexistence of sulfate and DMA ions in the pores, exhibiting the highest proton conductivity $(5.0\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ at $98\%$ RH and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [201]
The inclusion of strong acids such as sulfuric acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ and phosphoric acid $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4})$ into porous MOFs can greatly improve the proton-conducting properties of such hybrid materials.[206] The mesoporous MOF MIL-101, capable of withstanding such strong acidic conditions, was chosen as a host framework impregnated with either ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ to form the solid hybrid materials $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}{\cdot}101$ . The high proton conductivity was found to
## www.MaterialsViews.com
be $1\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ and $3\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}{\cdot}101$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and low humidity of $0.6\mathrm{\mol}$ $\%$ . The overall higher proton conductivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ strongly indicates that the conducting properties of hybrid materials depend on the $K_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of the guest acids. Such pHdependent proton conducting behavior was also confirmed by soaking Ni-MOF-74 into sulfuric acid solutions with various $\mathrm{\pH}$ values, where the conductivity at $\mathrm{\pH\1}.8$ reaches $2.2\times$ $10^{-2}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .[207]
If MOFs serve as proton conductors for practical applications, they must be able to operate at relatively high temperatures above $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and under anhydrous conditions. In this context, water cannot be used as a proton carrier. The synthesis of an anhydrous $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ conductor is much more difficult. An alternative strategy to fulfill anhydrous proton-conducting materials is the inclusion of weakly acidic guest molecules, such as triazole,[208] imidazole,[209] or histamine[210] molecules, into the empty pores as proton-carriers. In 2009, Kitagawa and co-workers reported the incorporation of the N-hetero­ cyclic imidazole molecules into the pores of Al(OH)(ndc) (ndc $=2{,}6$ -napthalenedicarboxylate) as proton-carriers.[209] The hybrid material shows a significant enhancement of proton conductivity from the order of $10^{-6}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to approximately $5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ under anhydrous conditions and $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Instead of using imidazole, accommodation of histamine into this MOF can further improve the anhydrous conductivity to $1.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .[210] This value is comparable to that of the best COF material $(4.37\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ with $164\mathrm{wt\%}$ of imidazole loading.[211]
Another study was carried out by Shimizu and co-workers,[208] wherein 1H-1,2,4-triazole $(\mathrm{Tz})$ was used as the guest proton carrier; this was partially loaded into a MOF, namely $\beta$ -PCMOF2. The partially loaded $\beta{\mathrm{-PCMOF}}2(\mathrm{Tz})_{\mathrm{x}}$ exhibit increased anhydrous proton conductivities in the order of $10^{-4}\ \mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which are much higher than those for pure $\beta$ -PCMOF2 $(10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ .
## 4.3. Other Conductive Applications
Porous MOF materials also hold the significant advantage of being widely applicable to newly developed energy storage and conversion systems, such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) and supercapacitors. In recent years, some MOFs have been used as electrode materials, electrolyte, or matrix for LIBs and supercapacitors.[212–216] ${\mathbb{W}}{\mathbb{u}}$ et al. developed several MOFs along with carbon black (Super P) as cathode materials to examine their performance in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries.[213] As shown in Figure 13a, electrochemical tests involving the discharge and charge of these assemblies were carried out in an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ –atmosphere glove box. A primary capacity of $9420~\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under 1 atm of oxygen was achieved in the Mn-MOF-74–Super $\mathrm{~P~}$ combination (Figure  13b). This value is more than four times higher than the capacities of Super $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ $(2170\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1})$ and carbon nanotubes $(\approx2500\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1})}$ ), but lower than that of the state-of-theart graphene electrodes ( $15000\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The accessible open metal sites in the uniform channels of MOFs are believed to enhance the population of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules in the pores, and assist the reaction efficiently towards high capacity. Their findings suggest that MOFs with open metal sites represent a viable option as cathode materials in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries.
Long and co-workers first developed a solid ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ electrolyte by incorporation of a lithium alkoxide within $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ .[214] This well-known framework consists of 1D hexagonal channels, and the pore interior possesses unsaturated $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ metal centers. These open $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ ions can preferentially bind the alkoxide anions, and the leaving $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ cations are relatively free to move along the channels. The resulting $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ conductivity was observed to be $3.1\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ on a pressed pellet of this material at $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This conductivity value is over the technological limit for a functional battery electrolyte $(10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S/cm})$ , but smaller than some crystalline ceramic materials, such as $\mathrm{RbAg}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{5}$ $(10^{-1}\mathrm{S/cm})$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{0.5}\mathrm{La}_{0.5}\mathrm{TiO}_{3}$ $(10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S/cm})$ . Yaghi and co-workers recently reported that nano-MOFs (nMOFs) doped with graphene can be successfully incorporated into devices to serve as supercapacitors for energy storage (Figure 14a).[215] After scanning a series of 23 different nMOFs with multiple organic functionalities and metal ions, differing pore sizes and shapes, and a variety of structure types, they found that a zirconium MOF (nMOF-867) exhibits exceptionally high capacitance with the stack and areal capacitance of $0.64$ and $5.09\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , about 6 times higher than that of the supercapacitors made from the benchmark commercial activated carbon and graphene materials (Figure 14b).
## 5. MOFs for Magnetic Materials
The exploration of molecular magnetic materials has attracted considerable attention because of their potential applications as future devices in high-density information storage and nanoscale electronics. MOFs, self-assembled from inorganic nodes and organic linkers, have shown great potential for the incorporation of paramagnetic metals as the nodes or paramagnetic organic ligands as the linkers to create magnetic materials. The mesopore nature of some mesoporous MOFs enables some single-molecule magnet (SMM) molecules to be encapsulated into the pores of MOFs for the formation of functional hybrid magnetic materials. Significant progress has been made on the fabrication of magnetic MOF materials in recent years by making use of the above strategies.[218–220]
Lanthanide ions have a large number of unpaired 4f electrons and strong magnetic anisotropy originated from unquenched orbital angular momentum. These features make such ions very appealing for the preparation of magnetic materials. A number of purely lanthanide-based Ln-MOFs and lanthanide– transition (f–d) heterometal-organic frameworks (HMOFs) have been explored for their magnetic properties.[220–223] Hong and co-workers found that a series of novel Ln-MOFs Ln(pqc) $\mathrm{(Hpqc)(NO_{3})_{2}}$ ( $\mathrm{{Ln}=\mathrm{{Tb}}}$ , Dy, Ho, Er; ${\mathrm{Hpqc}}=2$ -phenyl-4-quinolinecarboxylic acid) exhibit weak ferromagnetic coupling between lanthanide ions.[222] Cheng and co-workers reported the use of an N, O mixed donor ligand to obtain two Dy–Mn frameworks based on 3d–4f systems, both of which exhibit a rather rare ferromagnetic interaction between $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ions.[223]
Other paramagnetic metal centers, particularly the first row transition metals (V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni and Cu) are also extensively employed as the nodes for construction of magnetic MOF materials. For example, the significant singlechain magnet behavior was observed in two cobalt MOFs $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{0.67}\mathrm{bdt})_{3}{\cdot}20\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdt}\ =\ \ 5,\mathrm{5^{\prime}}$ -(1,4-phenylene)bis(1Htetrazole)) and $\mathrm{[Co_{3}(O H)_{2}(b t c a)_{2}]\cdot3.7H_{2}O}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{btca~=~}$ benzotriazole-5-carboxylic acid).[224,225] Férey and co-workers found that the nickel MOF $\mathrm{Ni}_{20}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{20}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}$ (MIL-77) behaves as a ferromagnet below 4 K.[226]
The introduction of paramagnetic organic linkers is one of the most promising strategies to support the formation of porous magnet MOF materials. Harris and co-workers used a paramagnetic tetraoxolene radical ligand (2,5-dichloro-3,6-dihydroxy-1,4-benzoquinone, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L})$ with $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ to construct a microporous magnetic solid $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot($ 6DMF.[227] Upon activation, the MOF undergoes a slight structural distortion to give the activated phase $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}]$ with a BET surface area of $885~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . It was revealed that the magnetic ordering temperature changes from $80~\mathrm{~K~}$ for the solvated solids to $26~\mathrm{K}$ for the activated solids, with magnetic hysteresis up to 60 and $20~\mathrm{K}$ . This strategy was also illustrated with $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{PTMTC})_{2}$ (pyridine) $_6(\mathrm{EtOH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\cdot10\mathrm{EtOH}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (MOROF-1) by using triphenylmethyl tricarboxylate $({\mathrm{PTMTC}}^{3-})$ as paramagnetic linkers.[228]
Due to the nature of their large pores, MOFs have the great potential to be excellent hosts to incorporate SMM molecules as guest molecules for the fabrication of hybrid magnetic materials. Wriedt and co-workers recently successfully encapsulated the SMM molecule $\mathrm{[Mn_{12}O_{12}(O_{2}C C H_{3})_{16}(O H_{2})_{4}]}$ $(\mathrm{Mn}_{12}\mathrm{Ac})$ into a mesoporous MOF host $[\mathrm{Al(OH)(SDC)}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (CYCU-3, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid) under mild conditions (Figure 15a,b).[229] The new magnetic composite $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn}_{12}\mathrm{Ac}\ @$ MOF fully retains its unique SMM properties with significantly enhanced thermal stability (Figure 15c). Similarly, Wang and co-workers incorporated a paramagnetic metallofullerene $\Upsilon_{2}@$ $\mathrm{C_{79}N}$ into the cage-shaped pores of MOF-177, where the $\mathrm{Y}_{2}@$ $\mathrm{C_{79}N}$ molecules inside the pores show axisymmetric paramagnetic properties.[230] These works pave a new way for the development of novel magnetic materials for high-density information storage devices.
Using the porosity of the frameworks, several groups have successfully tuned the magnetic properties with guest molecules.[231,232] Long and co-worker recently demonstrated that the magnetization dynamics of SMM nodes in a Ln-MOF, namely $\mathrm{Dy}_{2}(\mathrm{INO})_{4}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ ·2solvent $\mathrm{\IHINO=}$ isonicotinic acid N-oxide), can be tuned through guest exchange.[231] Binuclear $\mathrm{Dy}_{2}$ units of this MOF can serve as SMM nodes. Due to the porous nature of the MOF, exchange of guest molecules between DMF and acetonitrile can induce a reversible single-crystal to single-crystal transformation, leading to major changes in the magnetic relaxation behavior. The magnetic relaxation barrier can be tuned from a negligible value for $\mathsf{D y}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{D M F}$ to $76~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Dy}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ . This work not only provides a powerful new platform for tuning SMM behavior, but also opens a new way for future applications of porous magnetic MOFs as magnetic sensors.
## 6. MOFs for Chemical Sensors
Due to their porous nature, adjustable functionality, and unique optical, electronic and magnetic properties, porous MOFs have been explored as potential sensing materials.[233] The porous nature of MOFs can provide an ideal environment to accommodate analytes. The fine-tuning of pore/ window sizes within MOFs enables them to selectively take up and release specific guest substrates based on size-selective recognitions. Immobilization of functional sites such as open metal sites (OMSs) or Lewis acidic/basic sites can serve as specific binding sites, which initiate the specific coordination or hydrogen bonding interactions with target analytes to achieve the enhanced sensitivity.[19] The well-defined nanopores of MOFs offer the host–guest interactions for recognition of small molecules. In addition, some specific analytes and responses have also been utilized for the fabrication of MOF sensors. Although a summary of MOF-based sensors has been given in several review articles previously,[129,233] we still seek to summarize the various approaches reported for designing active MOFs as chemical sensors and to give an update on the recent advances in this research field simultaneously.
## 6.1. Size-Selective Recognition
Li and co-workers reported a microporous MOF $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpee})$ (bpdc $B=4,4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate; bpee $^{\circ}=1,2$ -bipyridylethene) for selective detection of trace explosives based on size-selective recognitions.[234] The fluorescence of the activated sample was drastically quenched more than $80\%$ within $10\mathrm{~s~}$ by analogues of explosive molecules such as 2,4-dinitrotoluene (DNT) and 2,3-dimethyl-2,3-dinitrobutane (DMNB) through a redoxquenching mechanism. The pore-size confinement of analytes inside the molecular-sized cavities can facilitate stronger interactions between the DMNB and the host framework, leading to the most significant quenching effect and thus the highest sensitivity. Similarly, Lu et al. exploited a ZIF-8 film that exhibits chemical selectivity for chemical vapors and gases due to the small portal size of the cavities.[235]
## 6.2. Specific Bonding Interactions
Rational design of MOF materials equipped with functional groups has shown to be another pathway for selective recognition or sensing applications. Given that the OMSs usually have a favorable interaction with analytes, the use of OMSs to achieve highly selective sensing has been widely explored. One of the earliest examples was realized by Chen et al. using a porous Eu-MOF with open $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ sites for selective sensing of solvent molecules.[236] The different binding interaction of open $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ sites with guest solvent molecules leads to the selective luminescence change toward various solvent molecules. Similarly, owing to the interaction of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with OMSs, Dinc˘a and co-workers revealed two MOF materials $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TCPE})$ and $\mathrm{Mg(H_{2}D H B D C)}$ for their selective detection of gaseous ammonia at elevated temperatures.[237]
Some research groups have succeeded in immobilizing Lewis basic sites such as free pyridyl or amide groups into MOFs for metal ion (e.g., $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+})$ ) sensing.[238–240] Recently, Cho et al. developed a pyrene-functionalized CPP-16 as an efficient chemosensor for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ detection.[240] As shown in Figure 16a, this material contains three important parts: a framework construction part, a $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ detection part, and a fluorophore part. After CPP-16 formation, pyrene moieties experience an unusual complete conversion from monomer to excimer form due to the limited space within the MOF. CPP-16 shows two characteristic excimer emission bands at 483 and $510\mathrm{nm}$ . Significant decrease of CPP-16 fluorescence intensity was observed upon addition of different concentrations of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , but not other transition metal ions (Figure 16b–d). The highly selective sensing of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ over other metal ions is mainly attributed to the nitrogen atoms of amide groups within CPP-16, which shows a specific binding of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to result in a second conformational change of the dangled pyrene groups from stacked excimer (CPP-16) to quenched excimer state $(\mathrm{CPP-}16\cdot\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})$ .
Xu and co-workers reported the use of the alkene unit in ASMOF-5 to bind noble-metal species preferentially for colorimetric detection of the noble Pd(II) ions.[241] Upon exposure to $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ in solution at a ppm level concentration, the color of the ASMOF-5 crystals is converted from light yellow to orange-red. However, other metal ions do not affect much the yellow color of the crystals. The unique color change caused by $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ makes it an excellent colorimetric sensor for $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ species. Following this work, they further realized that two MOFs with the thiol groups decorating the pores can be used for the monitoring of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions.[242] In addition, hydrogen bonding was also utilized by Chen et al. for the sensing of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ anion with a luminescent Ln-MOFs Tb(BTC)·G (MOF-76b, ${\cal G}=$ guest solvent).[243]
## 6.3. Host–Guest Interactions
The nanopore nature of MOFs provides a host environment to accommodate the analytes, which can certainly induce the host framework–guest interactions for selective chemical sensing. Quite a number of MOF sensors have been developed by taking advantage of host–guest interactions in recent years.[244–249] By incorporating a photoactive organic module NDI as a linker, Kitagawa et al. first reported an entangled porous framework, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dpNDI})$ $\langle\mathrm{dpNDI}\:=\:\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{\prime}$ -di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8- naphthalenediimide), as molecular decoding for sensing of aromatic volatile organic compounds (VOCs).[245] Upon incorporation of different aromatic VOCs, the activated framework shows a dynamic crystal-to-crystal transition in the framework entanglement to enhance the NDI–VOC interaction. The resulting host–guest interaction thus produces an intense tune-on emission, whose luminescent color is dependent on the chemical substituent of aromatic VOCs, and can be easily detectable by the naked eye.
Zhang et al. recently reported a 2D layered MOF (termed NUS-1) with wide channels and responsive turn-on fluorescence for chemical sensing of VOCs based on the host–guest interaction.[246] This framework consists of $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{{O}}}$ -like secondary building units and a tetraphenylethene (TPE)-based ligand, where this ligand has two dangling phenyl rings that remain unrestricted after the formation of MOFs (Figure 17a,b). The motion of dangling phenyl rings in NUS-1a can be restricted through molecular interactions with analytes to block nonradiative decay and trigger peak shifts, leading to responsive turn-on fluorescence toward different VOCs (Figure 17c).
In 2014, Maji and co-workers reported a flexible 3D MOF [Zn(ndc)(o-phen)]·DMF (o-phen $\mathbf{\Phi}=\mathbf{\Phi}1,10$ -phenanthroline) with confined nanospaces for recognition of different aromatic amine guests.[247] This material shows a characteristic visible color change and selective turn-on emission after encapsulation of different amines into the framework. Recently, Li and coworkers have demonstrated for the first time the use of a new luminescent MOF (LMOF-241) for selective detection of mycotoxins with the highest quenching efficiency $(K_{\mathrm{SV}})$ of $54227{\mathrm{~M}}^{-1}$ for any known sensory materials and a low detection limit of 46 ppb.[249] A possible detection mechanism via electrons, rather than energy transfer from the MOF to toxin molecules has been elucidated by theoretical analysis.
## 6.4. MOF-based Sensors for Specific Signal Response
To date, several luminescent MOF solids/films have been designed as oxygen sensors due to their potential applications in food science, environmental analysis, space research, and biochemistry.[250–254] For the current state-of-the-art MOF material, the luminescence intensity can be quenched $99.14\%$ by 1 bar $\mathrm{O}_{2},$ giving rise to a very large slope of $K_{\mathrm{SV}}=213$ bar−1.[253]
These values are comparable to the highest values of hybrid materials composed of metal complexes.[254–256] Qian and coworkers further reported the fabrication of MOF films for highly sensitive and fast-response oxygen sensors.[252] Two luminescent MOF films, $\mathrm{CPM}{\cdot}5{\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and M $\scriptstyle1\mathrm{I}\mathrm{L}-100(\mathrm{In})=\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ were constructed by introduction of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ into two indium MOFs. The $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ -based luminescence of both activated films can be gradually quenched by the increasing pressure of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The quenching efficiencies for $\mathrm{CPM}{\cdot}5{\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and MIL$100(\mathrm{In}){\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ films are about $47\%$ and $88\%$ at 1 atm of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Especially, MI $..100(\mathrm{In}){\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ film shows high oxygen sensitivity $K_{\mathrm{SV}}=7.59_{\it.}$ ) and very short response and recovery time for only about 6 and $53\mathrm{~s}_{\mathrm{{i}}}$ respectively, suggesting its bright potential for sensing oxygen gas.
Zhou and co-workers realized a water-stable zirconium-porphyrin MOF (PCN-225) for the sensing of $\mathrm{\pH}$ value.[257] This material exhibits exceptional chemical stability in aqueous solutions with pH ranging from 1 to 11. As a result of the wide$\mathrm{\pH}$ range stability and the presence of fluorescent porphyrin dye, PCN-225 shows a pH-dependent fluorescence. The fluorescence intensity of PCN-225 is in close correlation with $\mathrm{\pH}$ variation, of which $7<\mathrm{pH}<10$ is the most sensitive range for intensity response. The authors speculate that the pHdependent fluorescence of PCN-225 mainly originates from the protonation or deprotonation of the porphyrin core.
MOF thermometers have been realized by Qian, Chen, and co-workers based on a series of the mixed-lanthanide MOF systems.[258,259] A series of isostructural mixed-lanthanide MOFs $\mathrm{Eu_{x}T b_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotDMBDC}}$ $\mathrm{\Phi}(\mathrm{x}=0.0011$ , 0.0046 and 0.0069) have been specifically synthesized.[196] For the resulting mixed Ln-MOFs, the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ -based emissions gradually decreases as the temperature increases, while the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ luminescence increases due to the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}{\cdot}\mathrm{to}{\cdot}\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ energy transfer. Especially, $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.0069}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.9931}-$ DMBDC shows an excellent linear correlation between the temperature and luminescence intensity ratio $(\ensuremath{I_{\mathrm{Tb}}}/\ensuremath{I_{\mathrm{Eu}}})$ from 50 to $200~\mathrm{K}$ . The temperature-dependent luminescence colors can be systematically tuned from green-yellow to red from 10 to $300~\mathrm{K}$ with the sensitivity of $0.38\%$ per K, making great potential to be an excellent candidate for self-referencing luminescent thermometer. To utilize another organic linker 5-(pyridin-4-yl)isophthalate $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PIA}\right)$ with higher triplet state energy, Qian et al. further constructed a new MOF thermometer $\mathrm{Tb}_{0.95}\mathrm{Eu}_{0.05}\mathrm{I}$ IAwith the highest sensitivity of $6\%$ per K for any reported MOFs,[259] which is even much higher than other types of solid luminescent thermometers.[260,261]
## 6.5. Electronic and Magnetic MOFs for Sensing
While conventionally luminescent MOFs will be still extensively explored as various chemical sensors, the recent progress on conductive or magnetic MOFs has opened another door to develop some new types of MOF-based sensory devices.[262–265] Gassensmith et al. developed a MOF-based material with ionic conductivity for electrochemical sensing of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ within $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ or ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{Air}}$ mixtures.[262] CDMOF-2 was prepared by reaction of γ-cyclodextrin ( $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ , Figure 18a) and RbOH in aqueous methanol. Infusion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into CDMOF-2 resulted in covalent bonding of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to the free primary hydroxyl groups, forming alkyl carbonic acid (CA) functions on the $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ tori to yield CA-CDMOF-2 (Figure 18b). Interestingly, the ionic conductivity of pristine CDMOF-2, with a high initial value of ${\approx}4.8~\upmu\mathrm{S}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , was decreased by a factor of ${\approx}550$ -fold after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption to afford a very low conductivity of only $9\ \mathrm{nS\cm^{-1}}$ on CA-CDMOF-2 (Figure 18c) As depicted in Figure 18d, the conductivity of CDMOF-2 displays gradual responses to the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ present in a mixture with $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , which drops down sharply as the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration increases. These impressive features make this MOF as a sensor for quantitative sensing of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the presence of air.
Dinc˘a and co-workers demonstrated another new conductive 2D MOF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}]$ with a bulk conductivity of $0.2~\mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ that can be used for reversible chemiresistive sensing of ammonia vapor.[263] Recently, a series of conductive 2D MOFs were also constructed as a new class of materials for chemiresistive sensing of VOCs by the same group.[264]
Ferrando-Soria et al. reported the first magnetic sensing of gas and vapor molecules by a 3D mixed-MOF with the formula of $[\mathrm{Na(H_{2}O)_{3.25}]_{4}\{M n_{4}[\mathrm{Cu_{2}(M e_{3}m p b a)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3.33}]_{3}\}\cdot37H_{2}O}}$ $\mathbf{(H2O@iso1)}$ $\mathrm{(Me_{3}m p b a^{4-}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\Gamma~}=\mathrm{~\boldmath~\cal~N~},N^{\prime}{-}2,4,6~}$ -trimethyl-1,3-phen­ ylenebis(oxamate)).[265] The anhydrous iso1 displays not only a large selectivity in the separation of small gas ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ) and vapor molecules ( $\mathrm{\CH_{3}O H}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}^{\prime}$ ) but also a longrange three-dimensional ferromagnetic ordering. The magnetic ordering temperature can be simply tuned by different guest molecules. The values remain below $2.0\mathrm{~K~}$ for both $\mathbf{CO}2(\overline{{\alpha}}$ iso1 and $\mathbf{CH4}@\mathbf{iso1}$ but progressively shifted to higher values of $21.0\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\mathbf{CH3OH@iso1}$ and $\mathbf{H}2\mathbf{O}(\underline{{a}})\mathbf{iso1}$ . The coexistence of selective vapor and gas sorption behavior and the solventdependent long-range magnetic ordering temperature offers a fascinating potential for this material in magnetic sensing of small guest molecules.
## 7. MOFs for Heterogeneous Catalysis
Heterogeneous catalysis is one of the earliest established and extensively investigated applications for crystalline MOF materials. In comparison to zeolites, one of the most commercially important classes of catalysts, MOFs have some intrinsic advantages to develop as a new class of catalytic materials, such as ultra-high surface areas up to $7000~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ easily immobilized catalytic active centers into metal nodes or bridging linkers, and tunable pore sizes/sharps. The pore nature of MOF materials also enables us to encapsulate some catalytically active molecules or nanoparticles into the cavities of MOFs. These attractive features have led to a rapidly growing number of hybrid MOFs for heterogeneous catalysis.[266,267] In this section, we mainly focus on the recent progresses since 2012 on this very active field based on the following three different catalytic sites in porous networks: metal catalysis, organic catalysis, and guest molecular catalysts.
## 7.1. Metal Catalysis
In most cases, metal ions, such as $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , and lanthanide ions at the nodes have coordinated solvent molecules, which can be easily removed or replaced by substrates without losing the structural integrity of the framework. Such metal nodes with open coordination sites can be utilized as a catalyst in chemical transformations, especially as a Lewis acid catalyst. Recent works demonstrated that the incorporation of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ clusters into MOFs can serve as catalytically active sites for a wide range of chemical reactions. Another general methodology to immobilize metal catalysts into MOFs is to make use of metalloligands, such as metalloporphyrin, BINAP/BINOL, or chiral salen-based metal catalysts, as a bridging linker.
The use of metal ions (especially $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ or $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and carboxylate ligands to form a dinuclear metal paddle-wheel structure at the nodes has become one of the most common ways to generate active MOF catalysts.[268–270] One typical example is the well-known MOF HKUST-1. The coordinated water molecules can be easily removed to form open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites serving as catalytic centers. These Lewis acid sites could catalyze the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde or acetone.[268] In 2014, Ma and co-workers reported another copperbased nbo MOF, $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Cu}{\cdot}\mathrm{tactmb})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\right]$ (MMCF-2; $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{tactmb~=~}1,4,7,10\cdot$ -tetrazazcyclododecane- $\mathrm{N,N^{\prime},N^{\prime\prime},N^{\prime\prime\prime}}$ -tetrap-­methyl­benzoic acid) featuring a high density of cataly­tically active well-oriented copper sites. This MOF shows excellent catalytic activity with a yield of $95.4\%$ for the cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to epoxides at room temperature under 1 atm,[269] which is superior to some metal–salen-based porous organic networks.[271,272] In addition, the metal nodes of some iron-based MOFs can serve as catalytically active sites, capable of NO disproportionation or oxidation of ethane to ethanol at the presence of N2O.[273,274]
Given the known outstanding thermal and chemical stabilities, MOFs containing $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ clusters have been widely exploited as a new class of potentially catalytic materials. The Lewis-acidic ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ or ${\mathrm{Hf}}^{4+}$ ions can serve as the active sites for a variety range of heterogeneous catalysis.[275–279] $\mathrm{In~}2014$ , Farha and co-workers first reported the use of a new Hf-based MOF (Hf-NU-1000) for chemical fixation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under ambient conditions with $100\%$ yield.[275] After that, the same group demonstrated that NU-1000 with Lewis-acidic ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ centers is highly active for the destruction of the nerve agent simulant DMNP and the highly toxic chemical warfare agents (CWAs), such as Sarin (GB) and Soman (GD).[277] It was revealed that the extraordinary activity of NU-1000 is engendered by the Lewis-acidic $\mathrm{Zr^{IV}}$ ions as the active sites and their weak intermolecular interactions with DMNP and GD. The introduction of 6-connected zirconium nodes or acidic/basic sites into zirconium MOFs can further improve the catalytic activity for the hydrolysis of CWAs.[278,279] The best MOF-808 shows the very high hydrolysis rate $(t_{1/2}<0.5)$ ) for DMNP and DECP (diethyl cyanophosphonate), which is superior to homogeneous dimers and tetramers of Al-porphyrin-based porous organic polymers (POPs),[280,281] and comparable to the best polymeric poly­ niobate material (KGeNb).[282] For comparison, a POP containing Al-porphyrins moieties demonstrated a moderate hydrolysis rate of $t_{1/2}=90$ min.
Metalloporphyrin molecules have unique chemical, physical, and electronic structures and properties as excellent catalysts in many reactions and metallo-enzymology. The immobilization of metalloporphyrin catalysts into porous MOFs has been extensively explored to study their heterogeneous catalysis over the past decade.[283–286] For example, to improve the stability of porphyrinic MOFs, Zhou and co-workers recently used six-connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster as the nodes and metalloporphyrins for the self-assembly of a series of highly stable MOFs with 3D nanochannels, namely PCN-224 (no metal, Ni, Co, Fe) (Figure 19a–d).[285] The PCN-224 series exhibit not only ultrahigh chemical stability, but also high BET surface area of $2600~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . As expected, PCN-224(Co) exhibits high catalytic activity for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ /propylene oxide coupling reaction (Figure 19e), comparable to the homogeneous cobalt-porphyrin catalysts.[287] Very recently, Liu et al. used the porphyrin-containing MOF fb-1 as a photosensitizer to generate ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ using commercially available LEDs. The generated singlet oxygen was found to selectively oxidize a mustard-gas simulant, 2-chloroethyl ethyl sulfide (CEES) to the comparatively nontoxic product 2-chloroethyl ethyl sulfoxide (CEESO).[286]
Many metallosalen complexes have shown highly catalytic activity in the field of asymmetric synthesis, some of which have been utilized as functionalized building blocks to construct chiral MOFs for heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis.[288–291] As early as 2006, Nguyen and Hupp et al. reported the first chiral Mn-Salen-based MOF with large amounts of catalytic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ sites along the channels, exhibiting high catalytic activity on the epoxidation of 2,2-dimethyl-2H-chromene.[288] Lin and co-workers further realized that the rates of epoxidation reactions strongly depend on the MOF open channel sizes in a series of isoreticular chiral Mn-Salen MOFs.[289] The conversion rates increase in the order of increasing open channel sizes in this series with up to $92\%$ ee, a value that rivals that of its homogeneous analogues. In 2014, Cui and co-workers reported a new chiral porous MOF with dicarboxyl-functionalized chiral Co(salen) ligand as an efficient catalyst for hydrolytic kinetic resolution of racemic epoxides with high enantioselectivities up to $99.5\%$ ee.[291]
Another extensive development in this area involves the synthesis of BINOL/BINAL-based MOFs containing catalytically active metal ions, which have been extensively studied by Lin’s group in the last decade for a wide range of asymmetric catalysis.[292–294] A series of mesoporous chiral BINOL/BINAL-based MOFs have been prepared by Lin and co-workers in 2012.[292] After treatment with ${\mathrm{Ti}}({\mathrm{OiPr}})_{4}, $ the resulting materials are highly active asymmetric catalysts for the additions of aromatic aldehydes, whose enantioselectivities are strongly dependent on the open channel sizes. Recently, the first robust and chiral ${\mathsf{Z r}}{\mathsf{M O F}}$ based on a BINAP-derived dicarboxylate linker was also reported by the same group.[293] This MOF can be post-synthetically metalated with Ru and Rh complexes to afford highly enantioselective catalysts (Figure 20). The Rh-functionalized MOF exhibits 3 times as active as the homogeneous control and produces aryl addition products with ee’s of $>99\%$ , and the Ru-metalated material shows highly active in asymmetric hydrogenation of $\beta$ -keto esters and substituted alkenes.
Some other chelating ligands, especially containing nitrogen or oxygen donor ligands such as bipyridines,[295] catechol,[296,297] thiocatechol,[298] and salicylaldimine,[299] offer an interesting alternative to develop highly active solid MOF catalysts due to their coordinating ability with a wide range of metal ions and the ease of functionalization. For example, using a $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl-derived dicarboxylate bridging ligand, Manna et al. immobilized the $\mathrm{Ir/Pd}$ -functionalized moieties into UiO-type MOFs to afford highly robust and active solid catalysts though a postsynthetic metalation strategy.[295] It was found that bpyUiO-Ir shows excellent activity in both borylation of aromatic $\mathrm{C-H}$ bonds and ortho-silylation of benzylicsilyl ethers, at least 1250 times as high activity as the homogeneous control. The Pd-functionalized MOF can catalyze the dehydrogenation of substituted cyclohexenones under 1 atm $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with the yield up
