to $93\%$ , three times higher than the homogeneous control. Cohen and co-workers introduced isolated open $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites onto the strut of catecholato ligands within the UiO-type MOFs, resulting in unprecedented Fe-monocatecholato and $\mathrm{Cr}$ -monocatecholato species with open active metal centers.[296] The Crmetalated MOFs are active and efficient catalysts for the oxidation of alcohols to ketones using a wide range of substrates even under very low $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ loadings $(0.5{-}1\mathrm{mol}\%$ ).
## 7.2. Organic Catalysis
The utilization of catalytically active organic ligands to construct MOF materials has also drawn much attention for heterogeneous catalysis in the past decade. Since the discovery of the first homochiral zinc MOF whose organic unit embedded into the pores can catalyze an organic transformation in 2000,[300] great efforts have been continuously devoted to the development of MOF materials containing catalytically active organic ligands as solid catalysts in a wide range of catalytic applications.[301–305]
In 2009, Wang and co-workers realized a porous serinebased homochiral MOF that can catalyze 1, 2-addition of $\alpha,$ $\beta$ -unsaturated ketones.[301] Reaction of a multitopic chiral ligand, (S)-3-hydroxy-2-(pyridin-4-ylmethylamino)-propanoic acid, with $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ resulted in the formation of a 2D homochiral MOF with multitopic homochiral centers along the chiral 1D channels. This MOF can catalyze 1,2-addition of Grignard reagent to a range of other ketones (aldehyde) with the highest ee’s value up to $99\%$ . Control experiments indicated that $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ has no any catalytic activity, suggesting that the reaction was promoted by the organic functionality in the framework.
Using a newly designed free-radical ligand, Kitagawa and coworkers constructed a new free-radical-decorated PCP (FRPCP) with the formula of $[\mathrm{Cu(DPIO)}_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})]$ $(\mathrm{DPIO}~=~4,7{\cdot}\mathrm{bis}(4\cdot$ pyridyl)-1,1,3,3-tetramethylisoindolin-2-yloxyl) (Figure 21a,b), where the DPIO ligands are periodically integrated into the porous framework.[303] Single-crystal X-ray diffraction revealed a non-interpenetrated 3D framework with large 1D channels decorated with the nitroxyl catalytic sites (Figure 21c). Under an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or air atmosphere and mild conditions, this PCP proved to be an efficient, recyclable, and widely applicable catalyst for selective oxidation of various alcohols to the corresponding aldehydes or ketones in high conversions up to $100\%$ . Most importantly, the reaction conversion of FRPCP at the low $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{S}$ ratio was shown to be $>13$ times higher than that of the corresponding intermediate, 4,7-dibromo-1,1,3,3-tetramethylisoindolin-2-yloxyl (DBIO).
McGuirk et al. developed another efficient and robust MOF catalyst for F-C reactions between unsubstituted indoles and $\beta$ -nitroalkenes.[304] The immobilization of squaramide-based hydrogen-bond-donating (HBD) organocatalysts into the framework UiO-67 resulted in a mixed strut squaramide-functionalized UiO-67 derivative with the formula $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(S q u a r)_{2}(b p d c)_{2}}$ (UiO-67-Squar/bpdc). UiO-67-Squar/bpdc achieved the F-C reaction of $\beta$ -nitrosytrene and indole in approximately $80\%$ yield after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . However, no reaction products were observed in the presence of the free HBD squaramide catalysts. Therefore, the incorporation of the self-associative HBD organocatalyst into a MOF can “turn on” novel catalytic activity not seen in the free organocatalyst.
## 7.3. MOF-Encapsulated Catalysts
The pore nature of MOF materials facilitates the encapsulation of catalytically active guests into the cavities, making MOFs an ideal platform to incorporate catalytic centers. Many reports have appeared in recent years for the use of MOFs to encapsulate catalytically active guest molecules, such as porphyrins/metalloporphyrins,[306,307] enzymes,[308] phthalocyanine,[309] triphenylphosphine,[310] metal-organic polyhedrals,[311] and metal nanoparticles[312–314] for heterogeneous catalysis. At the same time, some novel strategies including templating effect,[307] single-molecule traps (SMTs),[308] dissociative linker exchange,[310] de novo assembly,[309] and hydrophilicity-directed approach[311] have been developed and utilized in order to encapsulate large catalytically guest molecules into the cavities of MOF materials.
The incorporation of porphyrins or metalloporphyrins into MOF cavities has been widely investigated. In 2008, Eddaoudi et al. utilized a robust anionic framework $[\mathrm{In}_{48}(\mathrm{HImDC})_{96}]$ (rho-ZMOF; $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{ImDC}=4,5$ -imidazoledicarboxylic acid) as a host for encapsulating catalytically active porphyrins and metalloporphyrins with high loading of $60\mathrm{\m}\%$ .[306] The Mn–porphyrin containing composite was tested as a catalyst for hydrocarbon oxidation with a total yield of $91.5\%$ . Subsequently, Zhang et al. used the templating effect of porphyrins to synthesize six tbo MOF materials with encapsulated catalytically active metalloporphyrins, which can serve as size-selective heterogeneous catalysts for oxidation of olefins.[307]
Recently, Zhou and co-workers developed a series of stable MOFs with rationally designed ultra-large mesoporous cages as SMTs for enzyme encapsulation (Figure 22a–c).[308] Of this series of highly stable SMT-containing MOFs, PCN-333(Al) shows the largest cage $(5.5\mathrm{nm})$ and one of the highest void volumes $(3.84~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ among all reported MOFs as well as high stability in aqueous solutions with $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ from 3 to 9. As a result of a high concentration of mesoporous cages as SMTs, three enzymes have been successfully encapsulated into PCN-333(Al)
with record-high loadings (Figure 22d). These immobilized enzymes show almost no leaching during catalysis and recycling, and maintain or surpass their catalytic activities over the free enzymes under harsh conditions.
Using bio-MOF-1 as a host framework, Ma and co-workers successfully encapsulated the functional metal(II) phthalocyanine molecules (M-Pc) into the framework via a new de novo assembly strategy.[309] Of these hybrid composites, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-Pc}\ @$ bio-MOF-1 was found to catalyze the styrene epoxidation reaction with a conversion of $72\%$ , a value higher than the homogeneous Co-Pc $(38\%)$ .[315] Very recently, Li and co-workers utilized, for the first time, a hydrophilicity-directed approach to encapsulate a large metal-organic polyhedral $(\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4})$ into MIL101, leading to the remarkably improved catalytic efficiency and stability in benzyl alcohol oxidation.[311]
The use of porous MOFs as supports to encapsulate metal nanoparticles (NPs) can afford the generation of MOF-supported metal NPs as catalysts for heterogeneous reactions.
