For example, $\mathtt{X u}$ and co-workers utilized a “double solvents” method to immobilize ultrafine Pt NPs inside the pores of MIL101.[312] The resulting $\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ composites exhibit excellent catalytic performances for catalytic reactions in all three phases. Subsequently, bimetallic Au-Pd NPs were successfully immobilized into MIL-101 and ethylenediamine (ED)-grafted MIL-101 by the same group for the complete conversion of formic acid to high-quality hydrogen.[313] In addition, Jiang et al. recently fabricated a Pd nanocubes $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ composite material, which can be used for the selective catalytic hydrogenation of olefins under 1 atm $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and light irradiation.[314]
## 8. MOFs for Photocatalytic Materials
Owing to the inexhaustible and sustainable energy influx form the sun, solar energy has been considered as one of the most promising alternative energy sources to solve the global energy crisis. Extensive endeavors have been devoted to realize how to efficiently utilize solar energy in the past few decades. One of the most efficient approaches is to harvest and convert solar energy to chemical fuels via various photocatalytic reactions, such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution, water oxidation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and organic transformations.[316,317] Inspired by light harvesting complexes in natural photosynthesis, numerous artificial light-harvesting MOFs have been constructed by immobilization of light-harvesting and energy-transport components into the frameworks.[318] Among these artificial MOF systems, efficient light harvesting and energy transfer can be observed. This indeed facilitates sunlight absorption and charge separation in solar energy conversion, making MOFs as efficient photosensitizers for photocatalytic reactions. Furthermore, catalytic active centers can be readily introduced into MOFs by incorporating onto either the metal-oxo clusters or bridging ligands. MOFs also provide an ideal platform to rationally organize the photosensitizers and catalytic active centers into a single solid system, served as bi-functionalized materials. Taking advantage of these intrinsic features, MOF materials have been recognized as having advanced potential to design artificial photocatalytic systems for photocatalytic water splitting, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and organic degradation and transformations.
## 8.1. Visible-Light-Driven ${\sf H}_{2}$ Evolution
Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution is an essential half reaction in water splitting, which can directly convert sunlight energy into the chemical energy of hydrogen molecules. Generally, a visible light-driven photocatalytic hydrogen evolution system requires two basic components–the photosensitizer to harvest sunlight and the catalyst to produce hydrogen using the harvested energy. Porous MOFs offer an intriguing potential to integrate the light-harvesting or/and catalytic components into the framework via pre-designed or post-synthesized methods. In recent years, a number of functionalized MOFs have been reported as effective photocatalysts for visible-light-driven $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, whose turnover numbers (TONs) can reach up to 7000,[319] a value that rivals some of the promising metal oxides,[320] transition-metal complexes,[321,322] and organometallic complex/semiconductor hybrid systems.[323]
To efficiently harvest sunlight, some light-absorbing organic and metal-organic chromophores, such as $\mathrm{Ir/Ru}$ complexes,[319,324] porphyrins,[325] and 2-aminoterephthalate,[326] have been utilized as building blocks for the synthesis of photocatalytic MOFs. In these systems, Pt nanoparticles are often employed as co-catalysts for promoting photoactivity. In 2012, Lin and co-workers reported the use of a light harvesting $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ derivative to construct robust $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs.[319] Pt nanoparticles were loaded into the cavities of MOFs to act as catalytic active centers. The resulting $\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ samples are highly efficient photocatalysts for hydrogen evolution under visible-light irradiation, giving three times higher TONs (up to 7000) than the corresponding homogeneous systems. Toyao et al. demonstrated the use of a Ru complex-incorporated Ti-based MOF for hydrogen production under visiblelight irradiation up to $620\ \mathrm{nm}$ .[324] In addition, introduction of a functional amino group into a Ti-based MOF backbone is capable of tuning the visible absorption band red-shifted to ${\approx}500~\mathrm{nm}$ , thus resulting in the significantly enhanced catalytic activity $(11.7\upmu\mathrm{molh}^{-1})$ .[326]
Another approach to obtain visible-light responsive MOFs is doping some light-absorbing metal complexes[327] or dye molecules[328] into the pores of MOFs. Yan and co-workers reported the doping of rhodamine B (RhB) dye into UiO-66 for enhanced photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production activity.[328] UiO-66 was considered as a photocatalyst due to the photoactivity of $Z\mathrm{r}$ oxo clusters, and RhB dye was used as a photosensitizer to absorb visible light. No appreciable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution was observed over pure UiO-66 because of barely any adsorption in the visible-light region. When the same amount of dye adsorbed into UiO-66, the resulting RhB/UiO-66-100 system with a strong adsorption at $554~\mathrm{nm}$ shows a photocatalytic activity of $33.9~\mathrm{\upmumol~g^{-1}~h^{-1}}$ which can be further improved to $116.0\upmu{\mathrm{mol}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ by loading Pt nanoparticles as a co-catalyst, a value comparable to some of the semiconductor-based photocatalysts and transition-metal complexes.[320,322]
Although MOFs containing Zr,[328] $\mathtt{R u}$ ,[329] Al,[325] or Ti-oxo[326] nodes have shown photocatalytic activity for hydrogen generation, they are not so highly effective, such that incorporatation of Pt or semiconductor nanoparticles[330] as co-catalysts is needed. Unlike the above mentioned examples, Pullen et al. explored a new photocatalyst UiO-66-[FeFe](dcbdt) $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ (dcbdt $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,4-dicarboxylbenzene-2,3-ditiolate) for hydrogen evolution by directly incorporating a [FeFe]-hydrogenase analog containing photoactive center $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dcbdt})(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ into UiO-66 via the postsynthetic exchange (PSE).[331] It was indicated that ${\approx}14\%$ of BDC in UiO-66 can be exchanged for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dcbdt})(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ . In conjunction with $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer and ascorbate as an electron donor, the resulting UiO-66-[FeFe](dcbdt) $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ shows high efficiency without adding Pt nanoparticles (about $600\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , three times better than that of the homogeneous adduct.
Porous MOFs also provide a great platform to rationally organize both the photosensitizer and catalytically active center into a single solid system serving as a bifunctional photocatalyst. In this regard, Xu et al. recently immobilized a platinum complex into a rigid framework Al(OH)(bpydc) (MOF-253;
bpydc $=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine-5,50-dicarboxylic acid) to construct a photocatalytically active MOF (termed MOF-253-Pt).[332] This functional­ ized MOF-253-Pt acts both as a photo­ sensitizer and a photocatalyst in the process of hydrogen evolution under visible-light irradiation. The photocatalytic activity of MOF$253\mathrm{-Pt}$ is approximately five times higher than that of the corresponding Pt complex. Such enhanced activity of MOF-253-Pt is probably attributed to the interactions of shortly separated $\mathrm{Pt/Pt}$ pairs and more efficient electron transfer within the porous framework.
## 8.2. Photocatalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
Photochemically reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into valuable fuels offers an attractive way to not only harvest energy from sunlight but also alleviate the rise of atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentrations. Recent studies have demonstrated the potential of using MOFs to fulfill photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into a wide range of small molecular species, including HCOOH, yields of $C H_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , CO, and so on.[261–271] $\mathrm{In}~2012$ , Li and co-workers reported the first example of using a photoactive Ti-containing MOF $\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(b d c\cdot N H_{2})_{6}}$ $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)) with permission.[341] for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to HCOOH in the presence of triethanolamine (TEOA) under visible light irradiation.[333] The introduction of - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups onto bdc linkers led to a significant red-shift in absorption band of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}.$ MIL-125(Ti) to around $550~\mathrm{nm}$ , serving as a photosensitizer to harvest visible light. The formation of the formate can be detected in the resulting products, whose amount reaches 8.14 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The redox-active titanium oxo-clusters of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-125(Ti) are believed to be the active species involved in photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The photocatalytic activity can be further enhanced by doping $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles into this framework.[334] In addition, the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -oxo clusters in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Uio- $66(\mathrm{Zr})$ can also serve as catalytic centers for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to HCOOH.[335]
Besides the amino group, the incorporation of metal-organic chromophores into MOFs is another common strategy to generate visible-light responsive photocatalysts.[336–339] For example, Luo et al. reported the incorporation of a highly efficient lightharvesting Ir unit to construct a coordination polymer photocatalyst $\mathrm{Y[Ir(ppy)_{2}(d c b p y)]_{2}[O H]}$ (Ir-CP).[337] The designed Ir-CP heterogeneous photocatalyst acts as both a photosensitizer and an active catalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Thus, this material shows remarkable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction activity under visible light irradiation, which can produce 38 μmol HCOOH in $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Very recently, Jiang and co-workers demonstrated that a porphyrin involved MOF (PCN-222) can be used to selectively capture and photoreduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to HCOOH with high efficiency $(30~\mathrm{\textmumol}$ in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ under visible-light irradiation.[338] In 2016, Kitagawa et al. reported a new PCP- $\mathrm{{Ru}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ -based catalyst, showing the high catalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO, HCOOH, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ even under a very low concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(5\%)$ .[339]
The integration of MOFs with inorganic semiconductors has been demonstrated to be capable of significantly boosting the photocatalytic efficiency for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[341–343] One of representative examples was reported by Xiong and co-workers using a hybrid material, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , for conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .[341] They fabricated $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ core-shell structures by integration of an inorganic semiconductor $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with HKUST-1 (Figure 23a). The designed hybrid material possesses an advanced structure with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to generate excitons, and a microporous core to capture gas molecules and provide sufficient surface area for photocatalysis. Under UV illumination for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion, this hybrid material shows a drastic enhancement in terms of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ yield and selectivity compared to the bare $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figure 23b). As revealed in Figure 23c, the photogenerated electrons can be effectively transferred from the semiconductor to the MOF cores to supply energetic electrons to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed on the MOF, followed by photocatalytic reduction to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with dramatically improved activity and selectivity.
Similarly, by growing ZIF-8 nanoparticles onto the surfaces of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ nanorods, the fabricated $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ hybrid nanorods show a significantly improved photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ due to the excellent adsorption property of ZIF-8 and photogenerated electrons of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ nanorods.[342] Wang and co-workers developed a new hybrid system by integrating the Co-ZIF-9 framework together with graphitic carbon nitride
## www.MaterialsViews.com
$(\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ semiconductor as a co-catalyst and a light harvester, leading to a high photocatalyitc activity in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion upon visible light illumination.[343]
Despite great progresses having been made in this area, the activities of MOF materials are still much lower compared to many homogeneous transition-metal compounds and heterogeneous photocatalysts based on metal-incorporated zeolites and semiconductors.[344–346] However, transition-metal compounds are not so robust and reusable, and the latter class of materials suffer from extremely low efficiencies under visible light irradiation because most of these systems only absorb UV light. Considering the versatile chemistry and good stability, MOF-based photocatalysts still hold some promising advantages on visiblelight-driven photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. We believe that with continued efforts on the design, synthesis and optimization of new MOFs, some competitive MOF-based photocatalysts will be targeted in the near future.
## 8.3. Water Oxidation
Recently, Das and co-workers reported another simple yet efficient approach to achieve water oxidation catalysts by encapsulating the molecular catalysts into MOFs.[348] The wellknown MOF MIL-101(Cr) with large well-defined cages was chosen as a host framework to incorporate the molecules of a highly reactive water oxidation catalyst $\{\mathrm{[Mn(tpy)]}_{2}(\mu{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{2}\}^{3+}$ (MnTD; $\mathrm{tpy}=2,2^{\prime}{:}6^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime}$ -terpyridine). The resulting material $\mathrm{MnTD{\subset}M I L{\cdot}101(C r)}$ achieved a more than 20-fold higher total TOF (about $5.0\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ) in water oxidation reaction compared with its homogeneous counterparts. Further study revealed that the TOF can be further improved to $40~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ using ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ as an external oxidant.[349]
Actually, the achieved highest TOF of $40\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for MOF materials is much lower than most of other catalytic water oxidation systems, such as heterogeneous inorganic oxide materials,[350] and molecular organometallic complexes based on different transition metals including Ru, Ir, Mn, Co and Fe.[351] For example, a $\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ complex shows a promising catalytic activity with a high TOF of $300\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , a value which is at least three orders of magnitude higher than that of the best MOF.[352] The insulating nature and poor catalytic activities of MOFs might be the main reasons for their extremely low performance toward water oxidation. Although electrocatalytic water oxidation using MOFs has also been studied over the past several years,[353] no significant improvement on the catalytic ability has been made in this field.
## 8.4. Photocatalytic Degradation and Transformations
Photocatalytic degradation of organic pollutants is one of the hot topics to address energy problems and reduce environmental contamination. A few MOFs have shown visible light photocatalytic activities for decomposing organic dye molecules, such as methylene blue (MB), rhodamine 6G, or methyl orange that are widely used as surrogates for organic pollutants.[354–357] Laurier et al. explored a series of iron(III)-based MOFs, containing $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}–\mu_{3}$ -oxo clusters, as visible light photocatalysts.[355] All the Fe(III)-MOF samples display clear photocatalytic activity for degradation of Rhodamine 6G in aqueous solution under visible light illumination, superior to the commercial $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ photocatalyst that shows no activity under the same conditions. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}{\mu}_{3}$ -oxo clusters are responsible for the visible light absorption and associated photocatalytic activity. Recently, Wu et al. also developed a novel $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{I/II}}}}$ -based two-fold interpenetrated MOF, $[\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}(\mathrm{DCTP})_{2}]\mathrm{NO}_{3}{\cdot}1.5\mathrm{DN}$ MF $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{DCTP}=$ $4^{\prime}$ -(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)- $4,2^{\prime}{:}6^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -terpyridine) for visible-lightdriven degradation of MB.[357]
Some research efforts have also been devoted to exploit the use of MOFs for photocatalytic transformations of organic molecules driven by visible light.[358,359] $\mathrm{In}2011$ , Lin and co-workers first incorporated $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ and $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ into the UiO67. The resulting MOFs show high photocatalytic activities in three organic transformations.[336] Wu et al. reported a tin-porphyrin MOF by immobilization of the photoactive Sn-porphyrins into the channel walls for photo-oxygenation of 1,5-dihydroxynaphthalene and sulfides under visible light irradiation. Almost quantitative conversions and remarkable selectivities (up to $>99\%$ ) can be achieved in heterogeneous phases with outstanding stability.[359]
## 9. Nanoscale MOFs (NMOFs) for Biomedicine
In the past decades, nanoparticles in the size range of $10{-}100\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter have been widely investigated as drug delivery and biomedical imaging agents. Recent studies have shown that MOFs can be scaled down to the nanoregime to form NMOFs using a variety of different techniques that have been developed for inorganic and organic polymeric nanoparticles.[360,361] NMOFs have some potential advantages over conventional nanomedicines, such as their structural diversity, high porosity, adjustable surface functionality, and intrinsic biodegradability. The particle composition, size, and morphology of NMOFs can be readily tuned to optimize the final particle properties. The inherent high porosity and mesoporous nature within some porous NMOFs might secure their very large loadings of drug and biological molecules such as anticancer drugs or proteins into their pores.[362–364] Up to now, there have been many reports of NMOFs for the use as imaging agents, as biosensors, and in drug delivery and therapy.
## 9.1. Imaging Agents
The feasibility of using a unique NIR–emitting NMOF as a NIR imaging agent in cells was first demonstrated by Rosi and co-workers with nano-Yb-PVDC-3.[365] The nano-Yb-PVDC-3 exhibits ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ NIR emission centered at $970~\mathrm{nm}$ in water and $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ Hepes buffer $\mathrm{(pH}\ 7.3)$ via antenna effect. The crystalline nano-Yb-PVDC-3 has a low $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ of $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ in certain specific biological media, which is stable enough to allow live cell imaging. After incubating nano-Yb-PVDC-3 into both human cancer (HeLa) and mouse (NIH 3T3) cells, the specific $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ emission signal was observed in the NIR microscopy mode with good sensitivity (1-s exposure time). Compared with the specific fluorescence arising from the PVDC together with the cellular autofluorescence in visible detection mode, the $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ NIR emission signal shows higher signal-to-noise ratio and more efficient discrimination due to the absence of cellular autofluorescence, making it a promising NIR-emitting reporter for imaging in living cells.
Because NMOFs can carry large amounts of paramagnetic metal ions, they have been extensively explored for use in magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). The first example of using NMOFs as contrast agents for MRI was reported by Lin and co-workers in 2006.[366] In this work, extraordinarily high relaxivities (35.8 and $55.8\ \mathrm{mM^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$ for $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ relaxivity, respectively) have been obtained for the Gd-NMOFs due to large amount of paramagnetic ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ ions carried by NMOFs. However, the in vivo toxicity of Gd-NMOFs is a concern. To overcome this issue, $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ -based NMOFs have been developed as MRI contrast agents based on the fact that $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ ion is much less toxic than Gd(III) ion.[367] These Mn-NMOFs are highly efficient $T_{2}$ -weighted MR contrast agents, showing the high $r_{2}$ relaxivity up to $\mathrm{141\mM^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$ . This value of $r_{2}$ relaxivity is very large compared to Gd-DOTA (a clinical magnetic resonance imaging), Gd-containing nanoparticle contrast agents, and other lanthanide nanoparticles,[368,369] indicating the potential of these Mn-NMOFs for $T_{2}$ -weighted MR imaging. As a comparison, Gd-DOTA and ${\mathrm{Gd}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ nanoparticles have a $r_{2}$ relaxivity of 4.9 and $28.9\ \mathrm{mM^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$ , respectively, when measured at 7 T.[370] Iron(III)-based PEGylated MIL-88 nanoparticles have also shown to be efficient contrast agents for $T_{2}$ -weighted MR imaging,[364] with a high $r_{2}$ relaxivity of $95~\mathrm{mM^{-1}~s^{-1}}$ under a $9.4\mathrm{~T~}$ magnetic field. In vivo MRI measurements on Wistar female rats after MIL-88 nanoparticle injection clearly indicate that enhanced contrast can be observed in the liver and spleen organs.
In addition, NMOFs have also been developed as contrast agents for optical imaging (OI)[371] and X-ray computed tomography (CT) imaging.[372,373] Dye-loaded nanoparticles and luminescent quantum dots have been widely explored as bright and stable contrast agents for OI. Lin and co-workers used the $[\mathrm{Ru}\{5,5^{\prime}{\cdot}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}\}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}]$ ligand and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ connecting points to construct phosphorescent NCPs for OI.[371]
The dye loadings of $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -NCPs reach $78.7\%$ and $57.4\%$ respectively. The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -NMOFs was further stabilized with a silica coating and functionalized with poly(ethylene glycol) (PEG) and PEG-anisamide for in vitro optical imaging of cancer cells. The significantly enhanced contrast and uptake were confirmed by laser scanning confocal fluorescence microscopy and particle uptake studies using H460 lung cancer cells.
Using 2,3,5,6-tetraiodo-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{I}_{4}–\mathrm{BDC-H}_{2})$ ) as bridging ligands and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ as the metal-connecting points, two iodinated NMOFs were synthesized for CT imaging.[372] These iodinated NMOFs carried very high iodine content (up to $63\mathrm{\wt\%}$ . As revealed by phantom CT studies, both NMOFs show higher X-ray attenuation coefficients $(4653\pm520)$ and $(4513\pm408)$ HU/M) than that of Iodixanol ( $3840\pm560)$ HU/M, a clinically used iodinated contrast agent) and other nanoparticle systems including $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}[374]$ and iodinated organic nanoparticles,[375] demonstrating their potential as contrast agents for CT imaging.
## 9.2. Biosensors
Compared to the countless examples of bulk MOF materials developed as chemical sensors, little was known about NMOFs for use as biosensors.[376–379] In 2007, Lin and co-workers demonstrated the use of a functionalized NMOF for the detection of dipicolinic acid (DPA).[376] A silica-coated Eu-doped NMOF $\mathrm{Gd}(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ was functionalized with a silylated TbEDTA monoamide derivative (Tb-EDTM) to yield a dual lanthanide biosensor for probe DPA. When excited at $278\ \mathrm{nm}$ this material only emitted $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ luminescence because the TbEDTM is essentially nonemissive. Upon exposure to DPA, the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ luminescence becomes clearly visible due to the formation of Tb-EDTM–DPA complex, while the $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ emission from the NMOF core acts as noninterfering internal calibration. Such a ratiomeric sensing scheme also works equally well in a Tris buffer solution, suggesting the great potential of luminescence sensing of DPA in biological systems.
In 2014, Lin et al. developed the first example of using NMOFs for real-time intracellular $\mathrm{pH}\ \mathrm{(pH_{i})}$ sensing in live cells.[377] By forming a thiourea linkage between the isothiocyanate group on FITC and the amino group of the bridging ligand, fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) was covalently conjugated to a UiO NMOF to generate F-UiO NMOFs. Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) imaging indicates that the F-UiO particles show pH-dependent ratiometric fluorescence changes in HBSS buffers with different $\mathrm{\pH}$ values ranging from 4.0 to 8.0, rendering as an excellent nanosensor for sensing $\mathrm{\pH_{i}}$ in live cells. Upon rapid and efficient endocytosis, F-UiO remained structurally intact inside endosomes. Live cell imaging studies revealed endo- and exocytosis of F-UiO and endosome acidification in real time. This report represents a new promising pathway by the use of fluorescently labeled NMOFs as nanosensors for $\mathrm{\pH_{i}}$ sensing in live cells.
Very recently, the same group, for the first time, reported the design of a phosphorescence/fluorescence dual-emissive NMOF (R-UiO), as an intracellular oxygen sensor.[378] This material contains a $\mathrm{Pt}$ -porphyrin ligand (DBP-Pt) as an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -sensitive probe and a Rhodamine-B isothiocyanate (RITC) ligand as an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -insensitive reference probe (Figure 24a). The loadings of Rhodamine-B could be adjusted to generate R-UiO-1 and R-UiO-2 that possess 0.6 and $1.6\mathrm{\mol}\%$ Rhodamine-B, respectively. Both R-UiO NMOFs show a strong emission at $630~\mathrm{nm}$ attributed to DBP-Pt and a weak emission at $570~\mathrm{nm}$ from RITC. The ratio of phosphorescence intensity $(630\ \mathrm{nm})$ to fluorescence intensity $(570~\mathrm{nm})$ in the absence and presence of oxygen, respectively, shows a good linear relationship with respect to $p{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ up to $80\ \mathrm{mmHg}$ (Figure 24b,c). In vitro experiments further confirmed the broad range and accuracy of using R-UiO as an intracellular $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ biosensor (Figure 24d–f).
## 9.3. Drug Delivery and Therapy
Because porous MOFs can exhibit exceptionally high surface areas with large pore sizes, they have been extensively used for the delivery and controlled release of therapeutic compounds in the last decade. The potential utility of porous MOFs for drug controlled delivery was first exploited by Férey et al. in 2006.[380] Due to the large mesoporosities, high ibuprofen loadings up to $60\%$ could be successfully encapsulated into MIL-100 and MIL101. This value is really remarkable, much higher than that for other mesoporous materials such as the ordered mesoporous silicas and COFs.[381,382] For comparison, two COF materials (PI-COF-4 and PI-COF-5) synthesized by Fang et al. have about $20\%$ ibuprofen loadings.[382] In 2009, Rosi et al. reported the first example of using an anionic MOF (bio-MOF-1) for the storage and release of an important cationic antiarrythmic drug.[383] Some flexible MOFs, like MIL-53(Cr, Fe), have also been used for in vitro drug delivery of ibuprofen.[384] In addition, several other MOFs have been readily formulated to deliver other important drug or gas molecules, including anticancer 5-FU,[385] methotrexate, caffeine,[386] and nitric oxide.[387]
In order for MOFs to be useful as efficient delivery vehicles for the delivery of drug molecules and chemotherapeutics in biological systems, MOFs need to be scaled down to nano­ meter sizes to form NMOFs. In 2008, Lin and co-workers have developed a new NMOF platform for the effective delivery of $46.7\%$ Pt-based drugs to cancer cells.[388] This NMOF was built by a cisplatin prodrug DSDP and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion, formulated as $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{DSCP})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{12}$ (NCP-1). Surface modification of NCP-1 with silyl-derived $\mathsf{c}(\mathsf{R G D f K})$ resulted in an increased cellular uptake and a lower $50\%$ maximal inhibitory concentration $(\mathrm{IC}_{50})$ value relative to free cisplatin. After that, the same group further realized an iron-based NMOF for cisplatin delivery.[389]
Horcajada et al. reported a series of non-toxic porous iron(III)-based NMOFs as superior nanocarriers for efficient controlled delivery of antitumoural and retroviral drugs against cancer and AIDS.[364] Four challenging anticancer or antiviral drugs (busulfan (Bu), azidothymidine triphosphate (AZTTP), cidofovir (CDV) and doxorubicin (doxo) can be efficiently loaded into these NMOFs with exceptionally high loadings. In vitro assays show that a significant anti-HIV activity was observed for (AZT-TP)-charged MOF nanoparticles.
Recently, Lin and co-workers reported the first use of UiO NMOFs for the co-delivery of cisplatin and pooled small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) to enhance therapeutic efficacy.[390] The cisplatin prodrug was loaded into the pores of UiO by encapsulation to form UiO-Cis, and siRNA was further loaded by coordinating to vacant $\mathrm{zr}$ sites on the NMOF surfaces to generate siRNA/UiO-Cis. The siRNA loading efficiency is as high as $81.6\%$ . The UiO framework is able to protect siRNAs from nuclease degradation, enhance siRNA cellular uptake, and promote siRNA escape from endosomes to silence MDR genes. As a result, pooled siRNAs/UiO-Cis shows significantly lower $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values in the cisplatin-resistant ovarian cancer cells compared with UiO-Cis $(4.2\pm0.6\$ vs $21.4\pm1.4\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for pooled siRNAs/UiO-cis and UiO-Cis). Annexin V conjugate staining provided further evidence for the apoptosis induced by siRNA/ UiO-Cis. The co-delivery of cisplatin and pooled siRNAs within NMOFs led to an order of magnitude enhancement in chemotherapeutic efficacy in vitro compared to single cisplatin therapy.
Photodynamic therapy (PDT) is an emerging treatment that uses a photosensitizing drug and red laser light to kill cancer cells. The mechanism is based on the interaction between the excited photosensitizer (PS) and surrounding molecules, generating reactive oxygen species, such as singlet oxygen, which will cause oxidative damage to diseased cells and tissues. Recently, several porphyrin-based NMOFs have been exploited as an efficient PS for PDT.[391–393] The best PDT efficacy of NMOFs can reach more than $90\%$ ,[392] comparable to most of clinically available PSs from the porphyrin family including Photofrin, Levulan and so on.[394] Lin and co-workers reported the first example of a Hf−porphyrin NMOF as a highly effective PS for PDT of resistant head and neck cancer.[391] Reaction of $\mathrm{HfCl}_{4}$ and a new porphyrin derivative, 5,15-di(p-benzoato) porphyrin $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DBP}\right)$ , led to a DBP−UiO NMOF formulated as $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{DBP})_{6}$ . After exposure to a LED light at $640\mathrm{nm}$ , DBP−UiO can generate ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , whose efficiency is at least twice than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DBP}$ . In vivo PDT efficacy studies demonstrated 50 times tumor volume reduction in half of the mice and complete tumor eradication in the other half of the mice treated with DBP−UiO. However, no therapeutic effect was observed in the mice treated with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DBP}$ . Very recently, Zhou and coworkers developed the use of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based porphyrinic MOF nanoparticles (PCN-224) for effective PDT (Figure 25a,b).[393] The size of PCN-224 nanoparticles can be finely tuned from 30, 60, 90, 140 to $190~\mathrm{nm}$ , and thus effects the PDT efficacy. Among these fractions, 90 nm-PCN-224 shows the best PDT efficacy of $81\%$ upon light irradiation of 420 and $630~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 25c), much higher than free TCPP linker (tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)­ porphyrin, $47\%$ . Postsynthetic folic acid (FA) modification on the surface of 90 nm-PCN-224 led to the FA modified 90 nm-PCN-224 for targeted PDT, where 1/4FA-PCN-224 shows the significantly enhanced PDT efficacy of over $90\%$ than unmodified PCN-224 under the same condition (Figure 25c,d).
Although the research on NMOFs for biomedicine is still in its infancy, NMOFs have shown great promise as novel nanocarriers for imaging agents, biosensors, and chemotherapeutics. With continued endeavors on the development of new NMOFs, it is anticipated that many other NMOFs as imaging agents, biosensors, and drug deliverers will be targeted in the future. The ability to combine targeting, drug delivery, molecular imaging, and treatment capability into a single NMOF particle will further facilitate the development of nanoscale theranostic devices for cancer diagnosis and therapy. Close multidisciplinary collaboration among chemists, materials scientists, biomedical scientists, and bioengineers will certainly facilitate the implementation of some promising NMOF materials and technologies for practical applications in theranostic nanomedicine.
## 10. MOFs for Other Emerging Research Areas
## 10.1. Polymer-MOF Hybrid Materials
Recently, the development of porous polymer-MOF hybrid materials has become an emerging research area in the MOF community. In 2015, for the first time, Cohen and co-workers reported a new strategy to convert 1D, non-porous, amorphous polymers into 3D, highly porous, crystalline MOFs by the use of organic polymers as building blocks.[395] As shown in Figure 26, they used a series of polymeric $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ polymer ligands (namely pbdc-xe, $\mathrm{~x~}=\ 5\ –8^{\prime}$ ) with $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF to prepare polyMOF materials. The morphology and particle size of the resulting polyMOFs could be controlled by using different polymer ligands and changing the reaction temperature. The polyMOF materials show significantly increased hydrophobicity with an increasing number of methylene spacers in the polymer ligands. Due to the incorporated polymer chains, these polyMOFs possess the smaller pore sizes than the original IRMOF-1, thus resulting in the notably enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities.
After this work, the same group further used a series of linear, flexible, chain polymer−ligands (pbdc-xa, $\mathbf{x}=9\mathbf{-}12$ ) with simple pillaring pyridine linkers to prepare new types of polyMOFs.[127] Owing to the hydrophobicity of polymer ligands, as well as the cross-linking of the polymer chains within the MOF lattice, the resulting polyMOFs demonstrated not only excellent water stability but also high adsorption selectivities for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\Nu_{2}$ , making them promising for capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from industrial flue gases. Their findings of significantly improved water stability and promising performance on gas separation may motivate more extensive research endeavors to develop some new polyMOFs for various applications.
## 10.2. Multivariate MOFs (MTV-MOFs)
The concept of MTV-MOFs, as one of the simple strategies to introduce heterogeneity within MOFs, was first proposed and demonstrated by Yaghi and co-workers, which enables two or more types of functionalities to be incorporated into a single MOF.[23] $\mathrm{~In~}2010$ , eighteen MTV-MOF-5 type structures were synthesized using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ and its derivatives with various functional groups: - $\cdot\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , -Br, - $(\mathrm{Cl})_{2}$ , $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ , - $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)_{2}$ , $\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C_{4}H_{4}}$ , - $\langle\mathrm{OC}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5}\rangle_{2}.$ and - $\cdot(\mathrm{OC}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{7})_{2}$ . In these MTV-MOFs, the backbone structure is ordered while the immobilized functional groups are varied and disordered. One of these MTV-MOFs contains eight distinct functionalities in one phase. The incorporation of several functional groups within the pores of MTV-MOFs leads to enhanced hydrogen uptake capacities and selectivities for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}$ separation compared with the best pure MOFs, which are not simple linear combinations of their constituents. The similar enhancement on gas adsorptive properties was also observed by the introduction of multiple types of functionalized tritopic linkers into MOF-177.[396] In addition, Li et al. demonstrated that the incorporation of pyrimidine-functionalized organic linker into NOTT-101 structure results in a series of MTV-MOFs with significantly improved methane storage capacities at room temperature.[53]
The multivariate MOF approach also enables chemists to incorporate some specific organic components or metalloligands that contain diverse physical and chemical properties and thus to engineer MOF materials with specific properties. For example, Yu et al. incorporated a photoactive ligand into ZJU-56 through this multivariate strategy, resulting in a twophoton responsive MOF that can be used for 3D patterning and two-photon imaging.[173] Also using the multivariate approach, some photocatalytic active building blocks can be immobilized onto porous MOFs to afford photocatalytic materials for hydrogen evolution,[331] water oxidation,[347] and carbon dioxide reduction.[346] Yaghi and co-workers reported that the control of surface functionalities by doping two functional groups, sulfonic acid $(-\mathrm{{SO}_{3}H}$ , S) and ammonium $\mathrm{(-N{H_{3}}^{+}}$ , N) into UiO-66 via the multivariate approach can finely tune the activity and selectivity of catalytic reactions.[397] In fact, this simple and versatile approach has been widely used in various MOF research fields so far, and will certainly continue to be a powerful tool for the community to introduce desirable functionalities into MOF materials.
## 10.3. Defect Engineering
Recently, defect engineering has been established as a powerful strategy to introduce random/ordered defects into the parent MOF systems for unusual heterogeneous structures and properties.[398–400] Although it remains a big challenge to structurally characterize how the defects are distributed, this strategy still opens up new potentials not only in gas sorption and separation, but also in other more challenging physical properties such as catalytic, magnetic, and electrical/conductive properties. In 2013, Zhou and co-workers incorporated a significant amount of missing-linker defects into the UiO-66 framework by using the acetic acid modulator.[401] They provided, for the first time, direct structural evidence for the existence of linker vacancies in UiO-66. The linker vacancies can be tuned systematically, resulting in notably improved porosities in the defective MOFs. As a result, the defective MOFs showed a significant enhancement of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ storage capacities at high pressure compared to the parent UiO-66. Similarly, Zhou and co-workers utilized a series of functionalized R-mBDC linkers to partially replace the TPTC linkers in the synthesis of PCN-125 $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(T P T C)]}$ $\begin{array}{l l l}{{\langle\mathrm{TPTC}}}&{{=}}&{{1,1^{\prime};4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}}}\end{array}$ -terphenyl- $3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}$ -tetracar­ boxylate).[402] The presence of R-mBDC linkers created a large number of defect sites in the PCN-125 framework, leading to defective MOFs with increased pores and functionalization for remarkably enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities than the pristine PCN-125. In contrast to the above linker defects, Hupp and co-workers recently realized that the introduction of “missing node” type defects into HKUST-1 by using isophtalic acid can finely tune gas sorption properties.[403]
Besides gas storage and separation properties, the defect structures also play an important role in exploiting some new challenging physical properties in MOFs, such as catalytic, magnetic, electrical, and luminescent properties.[398] $\mathrm{In}2014$ , Fang et al. prepared a series of defect-engineered MOFs (DEMOFs) by doping defective mBDC linkers (e.g., mBDC, $\Nu{\cal O}_{2}$ -mBDC, CNmBDC, OH-mBDC, and 3,5-pyridine-dicarboxylate (pydc)) into HKUST-1 (Figure 27a).[404] As revealed by the IR data in combination with accurate DFT calculations, they found that the electronic and steric properties at open metal sites can be finely modified by the tunable defect degree in these DEMOFs, thus leading to promising modified physical properties including band gap, magnetism, and porosity (Figure $27\mathrm{b,c)}$ . Also from the same group, they introduced and characterized structural defects into an isoreticular Ru analogue of HKUST-1 by doping pydc linkers.[405] The incorporation of defective pydc linker induced the partial reduction of Ru sites along with the formation of new open metal sites. Such electronic modification of Ru sites is mainly responsible for the enhanced catalytic activity in the formation of hydride species and catalytic hydrogenation of olefins compared to the parent Ru-MOF.
Although the creation of defects in MOFs brings a lot of benefits on diversiform properties, the identification and characterization of defects, on the molecular level, in MOFs still remain a grand challenge. Recently, there has been significant progress on this project, where such defects in MOFs can be characterized by a combination of techniques such as electron and fluorescence microscopy, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, and powder X-ray diffraction.[398] Recently, Yaghi and coworkers used single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SXRD) to firstly identify the precise structure of defects at the molecular level in the zirconium-based MOF UiO-66.[406] It was directly identified that the missing linker defect sites in UiO-66 are occupied by water coordinated directly to the zirconium centers, and charge balancing is achieved by hydroxide anions hydrogen bonding to the $\upmu^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}$ groups of the metal cluster.
Recent studies realized that it is possible to create ordered vacancies in MOFs at specific locations with controlled distributions. In 2014, Li and co-workers reported the creation of ordered metal vacancies and linker vacancies in a cubic MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{Py}\mathrm{C})_{3}]$ without destroying the structural integrity.[407] After removing a quarter of the metal ions and half of the linkers, the ordered vacancies could be produced though single-crystal to single-crystal transformation, accompanied by the formation of rectangular channels with increased pore sizes. Such defective MOFs with expanded pore sizes allow large dye molecules (acridine red) to fit into the pores, while no dye inclusion can be observed in the pristine MOF without vacancies. Furthermore, these ordered metal and linker vacancies can be further filled by the introduction of new metals $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\right.}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{La}^{3+}$ ) and new linkers ( $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -PyC and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - PyC) to rebuild the cubic MOF structure, respectively. This report demonstrated that ordered vacancies can be successfully generated in MOF materials for engineering some specific properties.
## 10.4. MOFs as Precursors/Templates for Nanocarbon Materials
Porous MOF materials have been widely utilized as precursors and/or templates for the synthesis of porous carbon and metal/carbon nano-materials, generating a variety of promising electrocatalysts for energy storage and conversion technologies including fuel cells, metal–air batteries and water electrolysis.[408–413] $\mathrm{~In~}2015$ , Feng and co-workers used zirconium-porphyrin MOFs CPM-99( $\mathrm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ , Zn, Co, Fe) as precursors to generate porphyrinic carbons for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) catalysts.[409] Benefiting from the high porosity, large cubic cavity, and high thermal stability of CPM-99 (Figure 28a), the resulting pyrolytic products not only retain the cubic morphology of the parent framework but also contain porphyrinic active sites, hierarchical porosity, and highly conducting networks (Figure 28b), showing high electrocatalytic activities for ORR. Particularly, CPM-99(Fe)-derived material has the best ORR activity among these carbons, comparable to commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ( $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ , Alfa) in alkaline and acidic media (Figure 28c,d). Similarly, Xu and co-workers chose a highly porous N-rich ZIF-8 as a precursor to make N-decorated nanoporous carbons at different high temperatures.[410] These carbons show remarkable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation capacities as well as excellent activities for the ORR.
The use of MOFs as precursors to prepare metal/carbon nanoparticles as electrocatalysts for efficient hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) has also been developed.[412] Lou and co-workers developed a MOFs-assisted strategy to combine HKUST-1 with guest Mo-based polyoxometalates for synthesizing porous metal/carbon nanoparticles (denoted as $\mathrm{MoC_{x}}$ nano-octahedrons). Benefiting from the porous and robust structure of the parent HKUST-1, the resultant $\mathrm{MoC_{x}}$ nanoparticles display remarkable electrocatalytic activity for HER, whose performance is comparable to the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst $(40~\mathrm{wt\%})$ in basic media. Very recently, Xia et al. also reported the use of ZIF-67 particles as precursors to directly synthesize N-doped carbon nanotubes.[413] The resulting carbon nanotubes can serve as active bifunctional electrocatalysts for both oxygen reduction and evolution reactions, with electrocatalytic activities superior to those of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts.
## 11. Prototype Multifunctional MOFs
The last two decades have witnessed rapid development in the synthesis, characterization, and properties of MOF materials. There are more than 20 000 different MOFs being reported and studied so far for a variety of applications that we have summarized in the above sections. With more and more in-depth studies in the MOF community, we found that there are increasing multi-functional MOF materials that contain two or more types of functionalities in a single MOF. In this section, we provided several prototype examples of the use of a single MOF as a highly versatile and tunable platform for exploring multi-functional materials.
The well-known MOF HKUST-1, first reported in 1999 by Chui et al.,[414] has been widely investigated over the past two decades. Due to its moderate surface area and optimized pore sizes, this material has shown excellent performances in a wide range of gas storage and separation applications including $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ storage,[47,415] $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation,[65] and the separation of light hydrocarbons, $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ , etc., (Figure 29a).[78,416,417] After removal of the coordinated water molecules from $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ nodes, the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites formed can serve as catalytic centers for various organic reactions including cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde/acetone, cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and so on (Figure 29b).[268,269] Recent studies further demonstrated the use of HKUST-1 as a platform to develop some interesting composites that show some novel properties. For example, the incorporation of redox-active TCNQ molecules into the cavities of insulated HKUST-1 results in a highly electrical conductivity of $7~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 29c),[192] and the HKUST-1/ $\mathrm{\DeltaTiO}_{2}$ hybrid composite shows high activity and selectivity for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to methane (Figure 29d).[341] As indicated above, HKUST-1 is indeed one of the most promising prototype multi-functional MOF materials with broad applications.
MOF-74 series are also a class of promising MOFs that have been extensively investigated as a multi-functional MOF material for a variety of applications. Given their high density of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, MOF-74 series hold considerable promise for methane storage[45] and the separation of gas streams, including mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ ,[418] $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ,[110] $\mathrm{CO}/\Nu_{2}$ ,[116] paraffins/olefins[77] etc. Such open metal nodes of Fe-MOF-74 can serve as catalytic centers capable of oxidation of ethane to ethanol in the presence of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .[274] Long et al. also found that the coordinatively unsaturated, redox-active $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ sites within $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ can be also used for strong binding and slow triggered release of nitric oxide.[419] In addition, several groups further developed these kind of MOFs to be conductive materials, which can be employed as effective cathode materials or solid $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ electrolytes in batteries.[213–215]
Benefiting from the known outstanding thermal and chemical stabilities, zirconium/ hafnium MOFs (Zr/Hf-MOFs) have been widely exploited for a variety of applications in the past five years. One of most developed Zr/Hf-MOFs is NU-1000, first reported by Hupp and co-workers in 2013.[420] After that, this group used this versatile material for a wide range of applications including gas adsorption, heterogeneous catalysis, sensing, electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution, and removal of selenate/selenite from water. As shown in Figure 30, this framework is built up from eight connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ nodes and tetratopic 1,3,6,8(p-benzoate) pyrene linkers, generating four terminal-zirconium-ligated $-\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{-OH}_{2}$ groups per $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ node into exceptionally wide $(31\textup{\AA})$ channels. The incorporation of perfluoroalkane functionalities into the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ nodes results in the good performance on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture due to the presence of $\mathrm{C-F}$ dipoles (Figure 30c).[421] The Lewis-acidic $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}^{4+}$ ions can also serve as highly
