## 12. Conclusions
Here, we have summarized the recent, more important progresses on multifunctional MOF materials for their broad applications in gas storage and separation, optical, electric and magnetic materials, chemical sensing, catalysis, and biomedicine. After two decades of intense research endeavors, the potential of MOFs as multifunctional materials has been fully explored beyond our imaginations. The MOF platform can be considered as a complex enzymatic system whose functions can not only originate from their porosities, but can also be generated from metal ions/clusters, organic linkers/metalloligands, and a variety of guest species ranging from metal nanoparticles, metal complexes, organic compounds, and biological molecules. The research focus on MOFs has gradually switched from chemistry to materials perspective, namely, from their crystal structure characterization and topological rationalization to in depth materials functions.
Over the past several years, materials scientists, engineering scientists, biomedical scientists and industrial partners have been gradually involved in this very active field; new dimensions and regions of research on MOF materials have been explored.. For example, the integration of MOFs with nanomaterials/composites has led to some novel materials whose properties are superior to and beyond the traditional MOF materials.[426–428] The realization of the first protein-based metal−organic framework might initiate research on protein coined multifunctional MOF materials.[429] Ionic liquids have been introduced into MOFs to generate anomalous phase behavior.[430] The very intensive research endeavors have also focused on the development of MOFs as potential ferroelectric and multiferroic materials, which show the coexistence of both magnetic and ferroelectric ordering.[431–434] Commercialization of some prototype MOF materials by multi-national companies has certainly facilitated ongoing active research endeavors on multifunctional MOF materials. Close collaborations among scientists and engineers from many different expertises and industrial partners are needed in order to not only initiate novel ideas on the exploration of multifunctional MOF materials, but to eventually lead to some useful materials for real-world applications in the future. In this regard, MOFs for gas storage and separations are particularly of interest. It is foreseen that some MOF materials for the storage of methane and acetylene, and separation of light hydrocarbons will be implemented in ourdaily life soon. Most importantly, some newly technical approaches (such as SURMOFs[435]) have been well developed for the fabrication of MOF-based films and devices,[436–439] which will likely accelerate the use of these multifunctional
